{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex (D737) chose to shrink the format. Code refuses an answer only for these five defects:\n     4\t- a number misread;\n     5\t- wrong arithmetic;\n     6\t- a part above its whole, or added into it;\n     7\t- two forecasts of one quarter;\n     8\t- an own estimate passed off as the market's.\n     9\t\n    10\tIt also refuses broken structure. Each rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of `DESIGN.md`'s\n    11\tchecks becomes something the readers see, not a refusal.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Review scope (D737):** a counterexample that lets none of the five defects through, and breaks no structure, is a reader's matter,\n    14\tnot a finding. **Out of scope:**\n    15\t- the frozen ruler and `poc-v118`;\n    16\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n    17\t- the old run and its checker `calc_check.py`, which stay as they are, so the old run stays reproducible.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n    20\t\n    21\t| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n    22\t|---|---|\n    23\t| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n    24\t| scenarios, scenario membership, `range` summaries (C4) | one forecast per shared quantity; an outcome may be a range |\n    25\t| relations `time_aggregation` / `accounting_parts` / `stated_in_addition` / `overlap_unknown`, and `disputed` | periods of one quantity must make up the output's period (S8); an uncertain overlap is written as an estimate with its assumption |\n    26\t| bridges and `converts` (C8) | a conversion between quantities is computed or estimated as the analyst chooses; code records each computed figure whose inputs measure another quantity, and readers judge it |\n    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    49\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n    50\t\n    51\t**[52] Operation units (stated in `operation`):**\n    52\t- sum and difference: one unit in, the same out.\n    53\t- product: USD bn × x → USD bn; x × x → x; % × x → %; never USD bn × %.\n    54\t- ratio: same unit → x; USD bn ÷ x → USD bn.\n    55\t- share(whole, percent): whole's unit.\n    56\t- growth(old, new): same unit → %.\n    57\t- apply_growth(base, rate %): base's unit.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2-old. The rules as first written (superseded by the revision above)\n    60\t\n    61\t| # | Defect | Rule (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    62\t|---|---|---|---|\n    63\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number as the quote writes it (`quote_number`): with currency, scale or percent sign, or the words double, triple or twice. The figure's value equals it, in billions of dollars for money. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) is given in `qualifier`, and none is given when none is written. A written range gives low and high. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    64\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A range input gives a range: low and high, or their midpoint as value. Money is never multiplied by a percentage (use share). Arity is as `operation` states. | `operation` |\n    65\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, the part's forecast figure is at most the whole's: each end of the part's value or range is at most the same end of the whole's. The part's outcome is at most the whole's outcome. | the family header; `outcome_for` |\n    66\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's (Relations). When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity written as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    67\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | For each shared quantity the request lists, at most one figure has basis forecast. When a member's outcome uses that quantity, exactly one does, and every figure of that quantity the outcome uses is reached only through it (the forecast itself may be computed from guidance or consensus). | the family header; `basis` |\n    68\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | A figure marked in `reference_for` has, at any depth beneath it, no estimate and no forecast, and at least one source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    69\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    70\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique. `inputs` name figures and `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure depends on itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, no rung, no marked figure and no reference. | the field descriptions |\n    71\t\n    72\t**Recorded for readers, never refused:**\n    73\t- each estimate's assumption;\n    74\t- each computed figure whose money inputs measure another population, measure or scope than its output;\n    75\t- each growth applied to a base of another period;\n    76\t- each source qualifier.\n    77\t\n    78\t## 3. The wire schema, word for word (examples from no covered company, AGENTS §6)\n    79\t\n    80\t**[52] These descriptions replace the ones in the table below:**\n    81\t\n    82\t| Field | Description [52] |\n    83\t|---|---|\n    84\t| `figures` (array) | \"The family's calculation. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. Figures name each other by id; no figure may use itself.\" |\n    85\t| `id` | \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\" |\n    86\t| `quote_number` | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion'), given as low and high. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\" |\n    87\t| `qualifier` | enum adds `near`; \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n    88\t| `operation` | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; USD bn ÷ x gives USD bn); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure built on the part's quantity to one built on the whole's (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole only to give the rest. When its inputs are periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n    89\t| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n    90\t| `reference_for` | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure.\" |\n    91\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\" |\n    92\t\n    93\t**[52] The family header is rendered from the family's data** (no member id is fixed in code):\n    94\t- \"Relations: <part> is part of <whole>. For each of <compare periods> and for the outcomes, the part's lowest value may not exceed the\n    95\t  whole's highest. A sum never adds the part into the whole.\"\n    96\t- \"Shared quantities: …\" (unchanged list).\n    97\t- \"Outcome keys …, in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n    98\t- For O: \"<key>: the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it.\"\n    99\t\n   100\t**figures[]**\n   101\t\n   102\t| Field | Type | Description |\n   103\t|---|---|---|\n   104\t| (array) | | \"The family's calculation: every number an answer rests on, once. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\" |\n   105\t| `id` | string | \"Short and unique, such as F1.\" |\n   106\t| `what` | object | \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\" |\n   107\t| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n   108\t| `what.period` | string | \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\" |\n   109\t| `kind` | enum source, computed, estimate | \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\" |\n   110\t| `basis` | enum reported, guidance, consensus, third_party, forecast | \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\" |\n   111\t| `value` | number or null | \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\" |\n   112\t| `low`, `high` | number or null | \"A range, low at most high.\" |\n   113\t| `unit` | enum USD bn, %, x | \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\" |\n   114\t| `evidence_id` | enum of passage ids, or null | \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\" |\n   115\t| `quote_number` | string or null | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency, scale or percent sign and nothing else, for example '$4.5 billion', '$300 million', '40%', '-3%', 'double'; a written range ('$3–4 billion') in full, given as low and high.\" |\n   116\t| `qualifier` | enum none, more than, over, at least, about, approximately, around, roughly, nearly, almost, up to, less than, below, under, or more, or less; or null | \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n   117\t| `inputs` | array of strings or null | \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\" |\n   118\t| `operation` | enum sum, difference, product, ratio, share, growth, apply_growth; or null | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + …; difference(a, b) = a − b; product(a, b, …) = a × b × …, never money by a percentage; ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b; share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change from old to new; apply_growth(base, rate) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). difference, ratio, share, growth and apply_growth take exactly two inputs. A range input gives a range (low and high, or their midpoint as value). The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's; when its inputs are periods of one quantity, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n   119\t| `basis_text` | string or null | \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\" |\n   120\t| `rests_on` | array of strings or null | \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\" |\n   121\t| `outcome_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: exactly one figure for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n   122\t| `reference_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure from a shown passage, or one computed only from such figures and reported ones. No estimate or forecast anywhere beneath it.\" |\n   123\t\n   124\t**answers[]**\n   125\t\n   126\t| Field | Description |\n   127\t|---|---|\n   128\t| `view_id` | (enum, unchanged) |\n   129\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why, and nothing else.\" |\n   130\t| `rung` | \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\" |\n   131\t| `reference_unavailable` | \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\" |\n   132\t| `reasons`, `why`, `cites`, `pushes`, `theme_pushes` | unchanged; `why`: \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\" |\n   133\t\n   134\t**The family header (user message)**\n   135\t- \"Relations: v8 is part of v4: for each of FY2027 Q3, FY2027 Q4, FY2027, the part's forecast figure may not exceed the whole's, and\n   136\t  a sum or difference never combines the two.\"\n   137\t- \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure that uses the quantity takes it: …\"\n   138\t- \"Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n   139\t\n   140\t**The system text:** one change. \"a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario\" becomes \"a quantity the family shares\n   141\thas one forecast\". Every other sentence is unchanged.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 4. Build\n   144\t\n   145\t- **New files.** `calc_small.py` holds `from_answer(data, family)` (wire to internal, with S6/S7 marker errors), `check`,\n   146\t  `publish_decision`, `evaluate`, `upstream_of` and `key`, and returns `{errors, recorded, published}` as `calc_check` does.\n   147\t  `render_v13.v13s_schema` builds the schema; `judging-v13s.txt` holds the system text.\n   148\t- **The plan.** `paid_gate.item` takes a format. A v13s plan (`paid-plan-v13s.json`, pinned) uses `calc_small`. A plan without a\n   149\t  format uses `calc_check` and `strip`, so the old run's `results.json` reproduces byte for byte on a copy.\n   150\t- **Rehearsal and mutants.** The rehearsal takes the plan path. Its stub answers are written in v13s. Each rule S1–S8 gets a mutant.\n   151\t- **Size.** Characters and estimated tokens for the whole request, before and after, per item. The schema is expected to be smaller\n   152\t  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\n   153\t\n   154\t**Value × reader.**\n   155\t- `from_answer`'s output is read by `check`, `publish_decision`, `merge`, `card_view` and `normalise`. It uses the internal names\n   156\t  those readers read (`evidence`, `inputs`, `rests_on`, `outcome`, `reference`), and the dropped keys never appear.\n   157\t- `merge`'s unification of a shared forecast uses `evaluate`'s values with no bound.\n   158\t- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n   159\t\n   160\t**State × event:** unchanged from D731. A refusal is retried once, diagnostic only; the first attempt is scored.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   163\t\n   164\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   165\t\n   166\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   169\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   170\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   171\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   172\t\n   173\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   174\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   175\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   176\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   177\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   178\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   179\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   180\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   181\t  - \"over $300 billion\" with qualifier over;\n   182\t  - \"-3%\";\n   183\t  - \"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\";\n   184\t  - $1.2 trillion as 1200 USD bn;\n   185\t  - a range outcome overlapping its rung;\n   186\t  - one figure marked for two members (its own family);\n   187\t  - a computed external reference;\n   188\t  - an unresolved member;\n   189\t  - a conversion between quantities (recorded);\n   190\t  - \"not only still expects $20 billion\" as a source;\n   191\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%.\n   192\t- **A2, defects.** Each is refused by its named rule:\n   193\t  - **S1:** 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\"; a qualifier dropped; one invented; \"-3%\" as 3; a number not in the quote; a passage's\n   194\t    summary number; trillions as 1.2 USD bn.\n   195\t  - **S2:** a plain wrong result; a wrong end of a range; division by zero; money × %; a three-input difference.\n   196\t  - **S3:** a part forecast above the whole for a listed quarter; an outcome above the whole's.\n   197\t  - **S8:** company + Data Center in one sum; Q2 + Q3 labelled H1; FY − Q1 labelled Q4.\n   198\t  - **S4:** two forecasts of one shared quarter; an outcome reaching guidance for a shared quarter beside the forecast.\n   199\t  - **S5:** a forecast beneath a reference; an estimate beneath a reference; a reported-only reference; both a marked reference and\n   200\t    a reason; neither.\n   201\t  - **S6:** no outcome marker; two; a marker on an unresolved member; an outcome key mismatch; a rung outside the ladder; a rung\n   202\t    whose band misses the outcome; at the band's upper edge.\n   203\t  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n   204\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n   205\t  reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   206\t  - prose outcome, reference and evidence;\n   207\t  - unit spellings;\n   208\t  - value with range;\n   209\t  - a four-input difference;\n   210\t  - money × % through product;\n   211\t  - \"first half\";\n   212\t  - a passage id in inputs;\n   213\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   214\t\n   215\t**[52] Additions to Part A.**\n   216\t- **A1 adds:**\n   217\t  - the remainder company − Data Center = the rest;\n   218\t  - \"US$10 billion\", \"$96.2bn\", \"roughly twice\", \"between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion\";\n   219\t  - \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" as a range and as a midpoint;\n   220\t  - a point Data Center forecast beside a company range (S3 holds);\n   221\t  - a final range estimate resting on a computed point of the same quantity (S4 holds).\n   222\t- **A2 adds:**\n   223\t  - Data Center × 1x added to company (S8);\n   224\t  - an estimate resting on both part and whole (recorded, not refused, and the record asserted);\n   225\t  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n   226\t  - Q3 guidance used directly beside a Q3 forecast (S4);\n   227\t  - an estimate itself marked as the reference (S5);\n   228\t  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n   229\t  - a declared value within tolerance whose recomputed value crosses the rung edge (S6 on the recomputed value);\n   230\t  - a part whose recomputed lowest value exceeds the whole's recomputed highest, while its declared value does not (S3);\n   231\t  - sum($108B, 2%) (S2 unit);\n   232\t  - division by [-1, 1] (S2);\n   233\t  - [-3, -2] × [2, 4] declared other than [-12, -4] (S2);\n   234\t  - a source as the outcome (S6);\n   235\t  - a figure id equal to a passage id (S7);\n   236\t  - \"plus or minus 2%\" copied as \"2%\" and used as +2% for the range (S1);\n   237\t  - a euro amount (S1).\n   238\t- **A3:** each mistake must be refused by the schema or **actually refused** by its stated rule (asserted). \"first half\" is refused by\n   239\t  S8 when summed. \"1.05 USD trillion\" fails the schema, and \"1050 USD bn\" passes.\n   240\t- **A4, the end to end rehearsal.** One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and\n   241\t  recovery. For each named defect, the defective answer fails its rule while its paired correct answer reaches the reader's card. The\n   242\t  old run's `results.json` reproduces from its journals with the ledger snapshot saved inside it.\n   243\t\n   244\t**[52] Readers.**\n   245\t- **What they get.** Both readers get the same complete shown passages, with the writer's identity and the checker's decision masked.\n   246\t- **What they mark, on every first reply:**\n   247\t  - whether each forecast is supported, naming the passage or assumption that puts it in its band;\n   248\t  - any of D737's five defects;\n   249\t  - broken structure.\n   250\t- **The wording review is separate.** Refusals are revealed only after that marking, for the review of whether the request states\n   251\t  the rule.\n   252\t- **An accepted reply with a named defect** (marked by both readers, or by the third reader) is a stop.\n   253\t- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\n   254\t\n   255\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   256\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   257\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   258\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   259\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   260\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   261\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   262\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   263\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   264\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   265\t  be supported in one reply.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   268\t- an A1 form refused;\n   269\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   270\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   271\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   272\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   273\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   274\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   275\t\n   276\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   277\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n   278\t\n   279\t**[D742] Registered before the third run (council rounds 56–56c).** The re-run stopped on one S4 refusal of a sound answer\n   280\t(`BLIND2-RESULT.md`). The cause was the request: the system text said to carry \"the guided growth forward\", while the schema allowed a\n   281\tguidance rate only as \"a stated benchmark for an estimate\" and the header required every path to go through the forecast. S4 is\n   282\tunchanged (rounds 56 and 56b showed that both a blanket and a narrowed exemption let a reset to guidance publish). The request now states\n   283\tone rule in the system text, the schema and the header: guidance informs the pace; the forecast pace is recorded as an estimate that\n   284\tnames its benchmark in `rests_on`; the quarter's dollar value is the one forecast figure. The rules for the third run:\n   285\t- **The stop lines are unchanged.**\n   286\t- **The refusal review** records two judgements apart for each refusal:\n   287\t  - sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound?\n   288\t  - stated: does the exact request (system text, schema and user text) clearly state the condition?\n   289\t  A sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run.\n   290\t- **The readers' brief** keeps Alex's ruling-1 sentence and adds: \"Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated\n   291\t  assumption, for a second dollar value of the quarter.\"\n   292\t- **Adjudication:**\n   293\t  - Where the two readers disagree, two adjudicators, one per family, read the packet. The packet now includes the system text.\n   294\t  - A point is settled when both agree.\n   295\t  - A settled defect on an accepted reply is a stop.\n   296\t  - A split is disclosed, not a stop.\n   297\t- **What each outcome sends where:**\n   298\t  - a fresh sound refusal sends the format back to council;\n   299\t  - an accepted reply confirmed to carry two values of a quarter sends the publication rule back to council;\n   300\t  - only a complete run that meets every line goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n   301\t- **Part A for the change:**\n   302\t  - five new cases: the stated-estimate benchmark passes; four forms of the saved refusal or a guidance reset are refused by S4;\n   303\t  - one new mutant: S4's walk following `rests_on` must fail the stated-estimate case;\n   304\t  - `s4_replay.py` on the saved reply.\n   305\t\n   306\t**[D743] The blinded re-read of the third run (Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; council round 57), registered before any packet is built.**\n   307\t- **The one change.** The readers' brief and the adjudication header add the schema's range rule to \"broken structure\": for a range\n   308\t  outcome, \"a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons\" is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is.\n   309\t- **What is re-read.** The same 12 first replies of `judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22`. Writers are not re-run, and the request is\n   310\t  unchanged.\n   311\t- **Two blinded controls** are built from a copy of E's Claude reply. Its growth estimate becomes a range, giving an outcome of $36–43B,\n   312\t  which crosses the $40B edge.\n   313\t  - C-overlap names rung 3 and gives its reason. It must not be marked.\n   314\t  - C-disjoint names rung 4 ($20–30B), a band the range does not reach. It must be marked broken structure.\n   315\t  Controls are scored on the readers' marks, not on support.\n   316\t- **Blinding.** 14 packets under a fresh shuffle (ids Q01–Q14, key in `reread-key.json` beside, never in the packets). The readers are\n   317\t  fresh: one Claude subagent and one Codex session per packet. They see only their packet, never earlier votes, the key or the expected\n   318\t  result.\n   319\t- **Scoring:**\n   320\t  - The registered stop lines apply to the 12 replies exactly as before, with adjudication where the readers disagree.\n   321\t  - The repair fails if either control is read wrongly by both readers, or by the adjudication when the two readers split on it.\n   322\t  - Any registered stop on the 12 replies, including S losing its only usable reply, sends the result back to council.\n   323\t  - A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send, beside the third run's record and its disclosures.\n   324\t\n   325\t**[D744] The second blinded re-read (Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; council round 58), registered before any packet is built.**\n   326\t- **The one change: shared rule text.** Readers get the same rule material as adjudicators, from one source (`blind_rules.py`): the\n   327\t  writer's system text, every schema description, and the definitions, which allow what the format rules permit. Readers judge in two\n   328\t  steps:\n   329\t  1. check quotes and calculations against the format rules;\n   330\t  2. name the evidence for the assumption that sets the band.\n   331\t- **What is re-read.** The same 12 first replies, by fresh readers (one Claude subagent and one Codex session per packet), under a fresh\n   332\t  shuffle T01–T17. The key, with answer paths, is in `reread2-key.json`, never in a packet.\n   333\t- **Five controls**, each checked by the code before use (`blind_reread2.py`), with these expected readings:\n   334\t\n   335\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   336\t  |---|---|---|\n   337\t  | K-midpoint | P's Claude reply, guidance given as the midpoint 108 | no \"number misread\" on that figure |\n   338\t  | K-misread | the same reply, guidance given as 108.5 | \"number misread\" on that figure |\n   339\t  | K-overlap | E's Claude reply with a growth range ($36–43B) and consistent text, rung 3 with its reason | no \"broken structure\" |\n   340\t  | K-disjoint | the same reply, rung 4 | \"broken structure\" |\n   341\t  | K-unsupported | E's Claude reply, 140% growth that its own reasons argue against | supported = no |\n   342\t- **Scoring:**\n   343\t  - A control fails if both readers read it wrongly, or, when the readers split on its mark, if the adjudication does.\n   344\t  - The registered stop lines apply to the 12 replies as before, with adjudication where readers disagree. The adjudication packet now\n   345\t    resolves control answers through the key.\n   346\t  - Any failed control or any registered stop ends the re-read, and the result goes back to council.\n   347\t  - Both S replies' support votes and reasons are reported beside the verdict.\n   348\t  - A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n   349\t\n   350\t**[D745] The targeted test (Alex: \"批准针对性小测\"; a justified unresolved answer counts as usable, \"算合格\"; council rounds 59–60), registered\n   351\tbefore anything is sent.**\n   352\t- **Request changes** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the v13 request is untouched, its plan hash is unchanged):\n   353\t  - \"If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that\n   354\t    justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap.\" This replaces the former\n   355\t    unresolved sentence.\n   356\t  - \"discounted by default\" becomes \"discounted subject to the evidence requirement below\".\n   357\t  - The qualifier list adds \"estimated\" (cases 111 of 111, mutants 43 of 43, rehearsal 19 of 19).\n   358\t- **Readers.** Shared rule text (`blind_rules.py`), including the allowed qualifier values and the unresolved ruling. First readers'\n   359\t  step 2 asks:\n   360\t  - for a forecast, the evidence for the band-deciding assumption;\n   361\t  - for an unresolved answer, the gap left after the strongest passages.\n   362\t  Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   363\t- **Scope** (`blind4.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`):\n   364\t  - cases S, E and P-v8;\n   365\t  - 6 unedited first replies (a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only Codex session per case);\n   366\t  - 9 blinded packets (6 replies + 3 controls), each read by one Claude and one Codex reader: 18 first readings, plus adjudication\n   367\t    where they disagree.\n   368\t- **Controls**, each checked by the code before use:\n   369\t\n   370\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   371\t  |---|---|---|\n   372\t  | K-unsupported | the saved third-run E Codex reply, with an unsized 15% cut | not supported |\n   373\t  | K-unresolved-unjustified | P-v8 Claude made unresolved with a vague reason | not supported |\n   374\t  | K-unresolved-justified | S made unresolved with the specific gap | supported |\n   375\t- **Pass:**\n   376\t  - every S and E reply has a settled \"yes\" on its forecast or on its reason for leaving the answer unresolved;\n   377\t  - both P-v8 replies have a settled \"yes\" on a forecast;\n   378\t  - all three controls read as expected;\n   379\t  - the refusal and defect stop lines hold as before.\n   380\t- **Otherwise:**\n   381\t  - a support split that adjudication does not settle leaves the run inconclusive, and it goes back to council;\n   382\t  - any failure goes back to council;\n   383\t  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md && cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md && cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"     201 docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\n    1056 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\n    1257 total\n# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
