{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^.{0,14}D(725|741|743|744|745)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"10957:## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n11255:    D725's lines.\n11346:## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n11416:## D743 · 2026-11-22 · The third v13s blind run stops on the reader brief; the writer side is clean; council 57 asks Alex to authorize a re-reading\n11449:## D744 · 2026-11-22 · The D743 re-read fixes the range rule but stops on the midpoint; one mechanism (readers lack the writer's format rules); council 58 asks Alex for one more bounded re-read under shared rule text\n11484:## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in BLIND4-RESULT BLIND3-RESULT PAID-GATE; do nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in BLIND4-RESULT BLIND3-RESULT PAID-GATE; do nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The D745 targeted test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745] and scored by\n     4\t`blind4.py tally` (`TALLY.json`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL (registered line \"every S and E reply has a settled yes\")\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Case | Writer | Shape | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". All four readers, both families, said\n    44\t    not supported.\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 4. What the paid re-send would meet\n    65\t\n    66\tUnder the paid gate's lines (`PAID-GATE.md` §4), a published singleton with a \"deciding gap\" stops the run. A deciding gap includes \"an\n    67\tunsized adjustment decides its band\". Where the two readers disagree, the third reader is Codex. On blind4's evidence, a published S\n    68\tor E forecast built like these replies would very likely be marked by Codex, and the run would stop. An S or E answer held as\n    69\tunresolved is not published and cannot stop it.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 5. Not done\n    72\t\n    73\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. No request text changed after the registration.\n     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, third run after D742: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D742])\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP on one registered line, as written** (council round 57 confirms): \"an accepted reply with a named defect (marked by both readers …)\".\n     4\t- **R01 (S, Claude writer).** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on F14: the outcome is a range of $679.25–745B, and rung 4\n     5\t  ($700–800B) does not hold all of it.\n     6\t- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n     7\t  with the reason in reasons\". The answer gives that reason: \"the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4\". The code (S6) checks\n     8\t  overlap.\n     9\t- **The cause is my reader brief.** Its definition of broken structure reads \"a rung whose band does not hold the outcome\", without the\n    10\t  schema's range clause. Both readers applied the brief as written. No earlier run had a range that crossed a rung edge, so the gap was\n    11\t  not exercised before.\n    12\t\n    13\tEverything else in the run is clean:\n    14\t- **Writers:** 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, so there was no refusal review.\n    15\t- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n    16\t  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n    17\t- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n    18\t- **The D742 rule was followed by every P-family writer.** All six recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark, then\n    19\t  computed Q4 from it. None computed a guidance rate straight into the calculation.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    22\t`schema.json`. The tally is in `TALLY.json`.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    25\t\n    26\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| P (joint) | v4 416.3, v8 386.1, both rung 2 | v4 420.9, v8 391.0, both rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v4 | 419.3, rung 2 | 416.3, rung 2 |\n    30\t| P-v8 | 387.4, rung 2 | 388.1, rung 2 |\n    31\t| S (CY2026) | 679.25–745, rung 4 | 670.5, rung 3 |\n    32\t| O | 9–20, rung 1 | 16, rung 1 |\n    33\t| E | 37, rung 3 | 34, rung 3 |\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    36\t\n    37\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    38\t- R01 broken structure (both readers' marks), explained above;\n    39\t- R01 \"two forecasts\" (Microsoft backed out of the four-company tallies, a reader matter through `rests_on`);\n    40\t- two \"plus or minus 2%\" midpoint marks (R02, R09) and a rounding difference within tolerance (R08). In all, both adjudicators\n    41\t  rejected 8 marks covering 7 distinct allegations (F14 was marked twice);\n    42\t- R06's \"number misread\" claims on the $18B schedule and the Lancium tranche.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Support:**\n    45\t- **R07 (S, Codex writer) is settled \"not supported\".** Its rung rests on a 90% data-center share, while the passages put the share near\n    46\t  100%:\n    47\t  - e1947: more than $730B \"on AI infrastructure\" against $720–745B of capex;\n    48\t  - e2759 and e5376: NVIDIA's \"nearly $800 billion\" for the top five, written both as capex and as data-center capex;\n    49\t  - Meta's own figures: about 96%.\n    50\t  At 94% or more, the same $745B gives rung 4. The reply is accepted and publishable, but its rung does not follow from the evidence.\n    51\t  This is not a registered stop line (usable is counted per case, and S has R01). It is a real judgement error in an answer that would\n    52\t  publish.\n    53\t- **R06 (O, Claude writer) is split again** on the $18B reading: Codex says not supported, Claude says supported. This is the same\n    54\t  passage as in both earlier runs.\n    55\t- Every other forecast is supported by both readers or both adjudicators.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. What the test shows\n    58\t\n    59\t- **The writer-side contract holds.** In three runs, the request has moved from 11 of 12 accepted with a checker error (run 1), to 11 of\n    60\t  12 with a request contradiction (run 2), to 12 of 12 with no refusal (run 3). D742's rule was followed by all six writers it applies\n    61\t  to.\n    62\t- **The stop this time is in the measuring instrument.** The reader brief's definition is narrower than the format the writer was\n    63\t  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n    64\t- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n    65\t  kind of error the paid re-send is meant to measure in gpt-6-luna.\n    66\t- **Council round 57 found two more points** (`codex-r57.md`):\n    67\t  - R01, the only usable S reply, marks Zacks's $720–745B of *total* capex as the market reference for *data-center* capex. Claude's\n    68\t    reader noticed it in its ground but filed no named defect.\n    69\t  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n    70\t    sits in rung 2.\n    71\t- **Open disclosures:**\n    72\t  - O's $18B reading (split three runs running);\n    73\t  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n    74\t  - R07's unsupported share.\n    75\t\n    76\t## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n    77\t\n    78\tFresh readers re-read the same 12 replies under the corrected range rule. Two controls were mixed in, under a fresh shuffle Q01–Q14.\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The range repair works.**\n    81\t  - C-disjoint was marked broken structure by both readers.\n    82\t  - C-overlap was marked by neither.\n    83\t  - R01 (now Q07), the range across a rung edge, was not marked broken structure by either reader.\n    84\t- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\n    85\t  - Both readers marked \"number misread\" on F6: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as its midpoint, 108.\n    86\t  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n    87\t    or its midpoint as value\".\n    88\t  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n    89\t- **The class.** The readers' packet carries the user text and the answer, but not the format rules the writer was given. The\n    90\t  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n    91\t  (the range, the midpoint) come from this one gap.\n    92\t- **C-overlap's other mark is my error.** Both readers marked \"two forecasts\" on C-overlap because my control kept E's original text\n    93\t  (\"85% … $37 billion\") beside the new range. The readers were right to flag the inconsistency. It does not touch the range rule the\n    94\t  control tests.\n    95\t- **Judgement noise:**\n    96\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n    97\t    readers now.\n    98\t  - S's Claude reply (Q07) is split at both levels this time: Codex says not supported and upholds two marks; Claude rejects them.\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   101\t\n   102\t## 5. The second blinded re-read (D744, Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; `reread2-packets/`, `reread2-adjudication/`, `REREAD2-TALLY.json`)\n   103\t\n   104\tReaders now get the same rule text as adjudicators (`blind_rules.py`). There were five clean controls, under a fresh shuffle T01–T17.\n   105\t\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\n     1\t# judging-v13 paid gate: plan, harness and reading rule (registered before any send; D725, D729, D730)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Result (D733): STOP.** All 72 answers were refused, and $0.3677 was spent; see `PAID-GATE-RESULT.md`.\n     4\t\n     5\t## 1. What is sent\n     6\t\n     7\t`paid_gate.py plan paid-plan.json` builds 12 requests from the stored runs.\n     8\t\n     9\t| Unit | Arm | Members | Evidence shown |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| pair-old-1 (09-20: v4 total, v8 Data Center) | joint; separate-v4; separate-v8 | both; one; one | the union of both stored requests' evidence in view (237 items), in every arm |\n    12\t| pair-old-2 (v14, v9) | joint; separate-v14; separate-v9 | as above | 237 |\n    13\t| pair-old-3 (v3, v35) | joint; separate-v3; separate-v35 | as above | 260 |\n    14\t| single-O (old-2 v29, equity commitments) | single | v29 | its own request's (35) |\n    15\t| single-S (old-2 v6, four hyperscalers' 2027 capex) | single | v6 | 31 |\n    16\t| single-E (old-3 v41, FY2028 Vera CPU) | single | v41 | 15 |\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Each request:** judging-v13's system text (`judging-v13-draft.txt`, 4,373 characters); the family header with the exact outcome\n    19\t  keys; the evidence; the themes once. No earlier forecast is shown (D728). The strict v13 schema.\n    20\t- **The model:** poc-v118's re-judging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium effort, 16,000 output tokens).\n    21\t- **Draws:** three per request, so 36 first attempts.\n    22\t- **Retry:** a calculation the checks refuse, or a schema-invalid answer, is retried once with the reasons. A second refusal makes the\n    23\t  unit unresolved.\n    24\t- **What is scored:** the first attempt, on both arms (§3f). Retries are sent and recorded for diagnosis only.\n    25\t- **The separate arm:** its two first answers per pair and draw are merged and checked over the pair's family (C3, C4 across the two).\n    26\t\n    27\t## 2. Spend and stops\n    28\t\n    29\t- **The ledger:** one, at Alex's ceiling (`paid_gate.py send <folder> <ceiling> paid-plan.json` refuses any other ceiling).\n    30\t- **Budget:** the real budget transport and stop gate, which reserve each request's worst case before sending. After a stop, nothing\n    31\t  more is sent, and the remaining units are recorded as not sent.\n    32\t- **Estimate** (at `hivemind.model.PRICES`: gpt-6-luna, $0.10 input and $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as\n    33\t  characters / 4):\n    34\t  - a pair request is about 34–38k input tokens and a singleton 6–9k;\n    35\t  - the 36 first attempts cost about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n    36\t  - the transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request.\n    37\t  - **Proposed ceiling: $0.50.** It covers every scored attempt. Retries come last (§3g), so a ceiling stop among them leaves the\n    38\t    scored test complete.\n    39\t- **Sends:** one at a time, every first attempt before any retry (§3g). Each send has its own archive, so no draw is answered from\n    40\t  another.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. The $0 rehearsal (`paid_rehearsal.py`, `rehearsal.log`: 16 of 16)\n    43\t\n    44\t- **Clean run:** 36 first attempts, every HTTP body byte-equal to an independently built request, every unit published, the separate\n    45\t  arm reconciled in 9 of 9, the spend settled under the ceiling.\n    46\t- **Paths:**\n    47\t  - a refused calculation is retried, then published;\n    48\t  - two refusals give unresolved;\n    49\t  - a schema-invalid answer is retried;\n    50\t  - two separate answers that each pass alone are refused when reconciled (C3);\n    51\t  - after a ledger stop, nothing more is sent.\n    52\t- **Packets:** 27 packets with the arm hidden. The verdict is computed on synthetic readings: both arms good gives STOP (gain under 3);\n    53\t  a joint gain of 4 gives PASS; one published singleton defect gives STOP; a separate-arm defect alone does not stop; a missing\n    54\t  reading gives INCOMPLETE.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 3b. After council round 47 (`codex-r47.md`)\n    57\t\n    58\t- **[47.1] Reconciliation:** forecasts of the same quantity and scenario with equal recomputed values and bounds are unified before the\n    59\t  family's checks run. The duplicate's derivation is kept in results. Forecasts that differ stay as a conflict. Rehearsed: an agreeing\n    60\t  pair passes, and a disagreeing pair is refused (C4).\n    61\t- **[47.2] One authorization:**\n    62\t  - `paid-plan.sha256` pins the reviewed plan, and the sender refuses any other plan;\n    63\t  - `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/authorization.json` is created exclusively at the first send, so a second launch is refused;\n    64\t  - (unit, arm) pairs are unique.\n    65\t- **[47.3] Every attempt recorded:**\n    66\t  - a `started` line is written before the provider is called;\n    67\t  - checker failures and arithmetic errors are refusals, recorded with the raw answer;\n    68\t  - every planned unit has a final status (`finals.json`), even after an abort;\n    69\t  - a refused reservation never reaches the provider.\n    70\t- **[47.4] Readings validated:** exact packets and cards, two readings (A, B) per card with yes or no and a ground for each question,\n    71\t  and a third reading on every disagreement. Otherwise the verdict is INCOMPLETE.\n    72\t- **[47.5] Packets:**\n    73\t  - the question text and ladder;\n    74\t  - each passage exactly as the request showed it, including those named only in `rests_on`;\n    75\t  - declared figures with renumbered ids, the recomputed outcomes and the recorded assumptions;\n    76\t  - the cards a non-reconciled separate unit has;\n    77\t  - neutral decision labels.\n    78\t- **Rehearsal:** 27 of 27 (`rehearsal.log`). The $0 gate still passes (`gate-run27.txt`: 87 of 87), and the mutants are unchanged.\n    79\t\n    80\t## 3c. After council round 47b (`codex-r47b.md`)\n    81\t\n    82\t- **[47b.1] Reconciliation:**\n    83\t  - Two recomputed values are the same when their bounds match and each end agrees within 1e-9 relative. A floating-point copy\n    84\t    unifies; a real difference stays a conflict.\n    85\t  - The readers never see the unified calculation. Each card of a reconciled packet is shown with its own original answer.\n    86\t- **[47b.2] Completeness comes from the attempt records.** `answers.jsonl` is the one record of what happened. For each planned unit\n    87\t  and draw, the final status is derived from it:\n    88\t  - no started line: **not sent**;\n    89\t  - a started line with no finished line: **interrupted**;\n    90\t  - a first refusal with no retry sent: **retry not sent**;\n    91\t  - otherwise, the last attempt's decision, and a second refusal is **unresolved**.\n    92\t\n    93\t  `results.json` is written from these records in a `finally`, so an abort still leaves it. The verdict is INCOMPLETE for every\n    94\t  status other than published, held or unresolved. That includes a reservation or HTTP failure, recorded as **transport**.\n    95\t- **[47b.3] Packets:**\n    96\t  - Every attempt of every card is listed, with neutral labels and check codes only.\n    97\t  - Each card is its own derivation: the figures its outcome and reference rest on, renumbered F1.. for that card alone.\n    98\t  - Its recorded assumptions are mapped to the same numbers. A record that names a figure outside the card is left out.\n    99\t- **[47b.4] Binding:**\n   100\t  - `packets.md` and the key are each created exclusively, once. A second `packets` call is refused.\n   101\t  - The key carries the run's digest (`answers.jsonl`, `results.json`, `plan.json`) and the sha256 of `packets.md`.\n   102\t  - The readings file carries the sha256 of the packets it was read on.\n   103\t  - The verdict is INCOMPLETE when any of the three differs, or when a ground is not a non-empty string naming a passage (e…) or a\n   104\t    figure (F…) of that packet.\n   105\t- **Rehearsal:** 41 of 41 (`rehearsal.log`). The new cases:\n   106\t  - a floating-point copy unifies;\n   107\t  - a reconciled card shows its own derivation;\n   108\t  - every attempt is listed;\n   109\t  - a transport failure makes the verdict INCOMPLETE;\n   110\t  - an interruption during a retry leaves results, marks the unit interrupted and the rest not sent;\n   111\t  - a retry that was never sent is incomplete;\n   112\t  - the card view maps assumption records and leaves out other cards';\n   113\t  - a second packets run, readings made on other packets, a run changed after freezing, and null or foreign grounds are each refused\n   114\t    or INCOMPLETE.\n   115\t- **Mutants:** 13 of 13 caught, and the control passes (`paid_mutants.py`, `paid-mutants.log`). Each mutant breaks one of these\n   116\t  defences on a scratch copy. The plan is unchanged (sha256 `6f81dae5…`), and `calc_check.py` is unchanged since gate run 27.\n   117\t\n   118\t## 3d. After council round 47c (`codex-r47c.md`)\n   119\t\n   120\t- **[47c.1] Reconciliation, by the joint arm's rule:**\n   121\t  - Only a forecast of a declared shared quantity is unified, and only with the other call's forecast of the same quantity and\n   122\t    scenario when their values are the same.\n   123\t  - Duplicates inside one call are never unified, so C3 and C4 judge them as they judge a joint answer.\n   124\t- **[47c.2] The key only orders the packets.**\n   125\t  - Each packet's decision, cards and allowed grounds come from the run.\n   126\t  - The verdict renders all packets again from the run and the key's order, and requires the text to equal `packets.md`, the key's\n   127\t    sha256 and the readings' sha256.\n   128\t  - The key must map exactly one packet to each unit of the run. A decision written into the key is ignored.\n   129\t- **[47c.3] Abstentions and attempts:**\n   130\t  - Each card is labelled K1, K2, … and is itself a valid ground, so a no-figure abstention can be read.\n   131\t  - Every earlier attempt shows its own answer, with figures named T<n>.F…. An attempt with no parsed answer shows its recorded\n   132\t    failure as a neutral label.\n   133\t  - Check codes are not shown: a cross-member code (C3, or C4 across members) occurs only in a joint answer and would reveal the arm.\n   134\t    The reasons stay in `results.json`.\n   135\t- **[47c.4] A torn journal:**\n   136\t  - A line that does not parse is skipped and listed as damaged. Its attempt counts as unfinished.\n   137\t  - `paid_gate.py recover <folder>` rebuilds `results.json` read-only and sends nothing.\n   138\t  - The verdict is INCOMPLETE while any line is damaged.\n   139\t- **Rehearsal:** 48 of 48, three times (`rehearsal.log`). The new cases:\n   140\t  - two equal forecasts inside one answer, and two equal shared forecasts inside one call, are both refused after reconciliation, as\n   141\t    in a joint answer;\n   142\t  - an abstention reads with K1 as its ground;\n   143\t  - earlier attempts show their answers, with no codes;\n   144\t  - a key that maps packets to other units is INCOMPLETE;\n   145\t  - a decision written into the key changes nothing;\n   146\t  - a torn journal recovers with 35 units published and the torn one interrupted, and its verdict is INCOMPLETE.\n   147\t- **A rehearsal fault found and traced:** one parallel control run failed 47 of 48.\n   148\t  - The swap test exchanged P01 and P02. In about 2 of 26 shuffles, these are two draws of the same unit and arm.\n   149\t  - The rehearsal's draws answer alike, so their text is identical and the swap changes nothing.\n   150\t  - That is harmless to the verdict, which counts by unit, not by draw. The test now swaps two different units.\n   151\t- **Mutants:** 19 of 19 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   152\t  - Three missed in the first run and were traced:\n   153\t    - one was stale, because packets now render from the attempts;\n   154\t    - one was masked by the shared-only filter, and a shared within-call case was added;\n   155\t    - one disabled only half a condition.\n   156\t  - One mutant is equivalent and is left out: unifying any key across calls, not only declared shared ones. C3 looks only at\n   157\t    forecasts reachable from each member's own outcome, so the result does not change.\n   158\t\n   159\t## 3e. After council round 47d (`codex-r47d.md`): three structural rules\n   160\t\n   161\tRounds 47b to 47d kept finding the same two things: process information inside the readers' packets, and a mapping from packets to\n   162\tunits that could change after reading. These findings were answered by rule, not one by one.\n   163\t\n   164\t1. **The scoring text holds only what the four questions need.** For each card: the question, the ladder, the passages it names,\n   165\t   its own figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. It carries no attempt history, no code decision\n   166\t   and no check code. The verdict applies the code's decision itself.\n   167\t   - Earlier attempts go to `attempts.md`. That file is for diagnostic reading only and is never scored. Each attempt sits under its\n   168\t     own shuffled label (D01, …), with no link to a packet. [47d.3]\n   169\t   - A card with no final answer says why in plain words: no request was sent; the request was started and no answer was recorded;\n   170\t     no answer arrived; the answer was refused and its retry was not sent; or the answer did not match the format. [47d.4]\n   171\t2. **The text the readers receive commits to the mapping.** The key is {a random salt, the run's digest, the packet order, the\n   172\t   attempt labels}. Its sha256 is the first line of `packets.md`. The verdict refuses a key whose sha256 differs from that line. It\n   173\t   also renders the packets again from the run and the key, and refuses a mismatch with `packets.md` or with the readings' sha256.\n   174\t   A swap of two packets with identical text is caught, and so is a decision written into the key. [47d.1]\n   175\t3. **Reconciliation is one to one.** A declared shared forecast is unified only when each of the two calls holds exactly one\n   176\t   candidate for that quantity and scenario, and their values are the same. Otherwise every copy stays, for C3 and C4 to judge as in a\n   177\t   joint answer. [47d.2]\n   178\t\n   179\t- **The record:** an attempt that was started and not finished is kept in `results.json` as interrupted.\n   180\t- **Rehearsal:** 51 of 51, three times (`rehearsal.log`). The new cases:\n   181\t  - the scoring text has no attempt, decision or code;\n   182\t  - the refused first answer appears only in `attempts.md`;\n   183\t  - a swap of identical-text packets is INCOMPLETE;\n   184\t  - a forged key is INCOMPLETE;\n   185\t  - two equal shared forecasts in the second call are refused (C4, three nodes);\n   186\t  - an interrupted card says the request was started.\n   187\t- **Mutants:** 20 of 20 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   188\t\n   189\t## 3f. After council round 47e (`codex-r47e.md`)\n   190\t\n   191\t- **[47e.1] The first attempt is scored, on both arms.**\n   192\t  - In the joint arm, a cross-card conflict (C3) is refused and retried with its reason, so the retry can repair it. In the separate\n   193\t    arm, the same conflict appears only when the two answers are reconciled, after both calls are over, and there is no retry.\n   194\t  - Scoring the final answers would credit the joint arm with a second chance the separate arm cannot have.\n   195\t  - D725 registered both arms \"under the same format and checks\" and says nothing about retries. Its pass and stop lines stay as\n   196\t    written and are applied to the first attempts.\n   197\t  - Retries are still sent and read for diagnosis (`attempts.md`), and are not counted.\n   198\t  - Rehearsed: the joint pair is published after its retry, but its refused first attempt is scored, so both arms come out at 6.\n   199\t- **[47e.2] One unit.** A shared forecast is unified only when the two copies have the same unit, as well as the same value and bounds.\n   200\t  Rehearsed: 110 USD bn against 110 USD is refused (C4).\n   201\t- **[47e.3] Every returned text is readable.**\n   202\t  - When the format check fails an answer, the scoring packet keeps its neutral label.\n   203\t  - The text the model returned, and the passages it names, go to `attempts.md` under a blind label. This covers both a first attempt\n   204\t    and a final one that fails the format.\n   205\t- **[47e.4] One verdict per run.**\n   206\t  - `verdict` copies the readings into the run once (`readings.json`, created exclusively).\n   207\t  - It scores them and writes `verdict.json` once, holding the readings' sha256, the key commitment, the packets' sha256, the run digest\n   208\t    and the result.\n   209\t  - A second verdict on the run is refused. `paid_gate.py replay` recomputes from the sealed readings and must reproduce the result.\n   210\t  - Rehearsed: the second verdict is refused; replay reproduces the result; after the sealed readings are edited, replay fails.\n   211\t- **Rehearsal:** 55 of 55, run three times (`rehearsal.log`).\n   212\t- **Mutants:** 25 of 25 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   213\t  - Two obsolete mutants were repointed or removed.\n   214\t  - One mutant crashed on a missing file and not on the defence. The test was corrected, and the mutant now fails the right check.\n   215\t\n   216\t## 3g. After council round 47f (`codex-r47f.md`)\n   217\t\n   218\t- **[47f.1] Retries cannot decide whether the scored test is complete.**\n   219\t  - The run sends every first attempt (all 36) before any retry.\n   220\t  - Completeness is judged on the first attempts: each must end published, held or refused.\n   221\t  - A retry that fails in transport, is interrupted or is never sent stays in the diagnostic record.\n   222\t  - Rehearsed: an interruption during a retry leaves all 36 scored attempts and a complete verdict. A transport failure on a retry\n   223\t    leaves the verdict complete. An interruption during a scored attempt makes the verdict INCOMPLETE. In the journal, every first\n   224\t    attempt comes before every retry.\n   225\t- **[47f.2] Every returned text survives.**\n   226\t  - The record keeps the provider's whole response, as the archive stores it, with no 20,000-character cut.\n   227\t  - A null content is shown as the model's refusal, or as \"no text returned\".\n   228\t  - Both files are rendered before anything is written, and a failed render leaves no files.\n   229\t  - Rehearsed: a refusal with null content, a 25,000-character malformed answer whose passage is named at its end, and a render that\n   230\t    fails and then succeeds.\n   231\t- **Rehearsal:** 61 of 61, three runs (`rehearsal.log`).\n   232\t- **Mutants:** 30 of 30 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`). Two mutants are caught by the crash the defence prevents:\n   233\t  - without the interrupted branch, the summary fails on an unfinished attempt;\n   234\t  - passing a null content on, packet rendering fails.\n   235\t\n   236\t## 3h. After council round 47g (`codex-r47g.md`)\n   237\t\n   238\t- **[47g] Two journals, read on the writer's own delimiter.**\n   239\t  - Scored first attempts are written to `answers.jsonl`, and diagnostic retries to `retries.jsonl`. The run digest covers both.\n   240\t  - A damaged line in `answers.jsonl` makes the verdict INCOMPLETE. A damaged line in `retries.jsonl` is reported and marks that retry\n   241\t    interrupted, but leaves the scored test complete.\n   242\t  - Records are split on '\\n' only, the writer's delimiter. An answer may contain other Unicode line separators (U+2028, U+0085,\n   243\t    U+2029), and these are kept inside the record.\n   244\t  - Rehearsed: a torn retry record leaves the verdict complete; a torn scored record (the existing control) makes it INCOMPLETE; an\n   245\t    answer holding U+2028, U+0085 and U+2029 stays one record; the order of every first attempt before any retry is checked on the\n   246\t    provider's own call sequence.\n   247\t- **Rehearsal:** 63 of 63, three runs (`rehearsal.log`).\n   248\t- **Mutants:** 33 of 33 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   249\t  - One mutant (splitting on any line break) is caught when a scenario guard stops the rehearsal: 36 attempts started, 35 finished.\n   250\t  - The mutant harness now counts such a stop as a catch and shows its reason.\n   251\t\n   252\t## 4. The reading rule (fixed now, before any answer exists)\n   253\t\n   254\t- **Packets:** 27 (`paid_gate.py packets`): 9 joint pair-draws, 9 reconciled separate pair-draws and 9 singleton draws. The labels are\n   255\t  shuffled, the arm is hidden, and the key is kept in `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/`.\n   256\t  - Each packet shows, per card (K1, K2), the question, the ladder, every passage the final answer names (as the request showed it),\n   257\t    its figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. Nothing about attempts or the code's decision is\n   258\t    shown (§3e).\n   259\t  - A packet the code did not publish (held, unresolved, not reconciled) is read too. The verdict, not the reader, applies the code's\n   260\t    decision: its cards cannot count as usable.\n   261\t  - The readers receive `packets.md` exactly, with its key-commitment line. The readings file carries that text's sha256.\n   262\t- **Readers:** two fresh readers of different families, a Codex session and a Claude subagent, each blind to the other and to the arm.\n   263\t  For each card each answers four questions, yes or no, with the passage that decides:\n   264\t  1. **usable:** the outcome is usable and supported by the shown passages it rests on;\n   265\t  2. **contradiction:** with the other card of its packet, it states a hard numerical contradiction;\n   266\t  3. **internal_reference:** the system's own forecast is presented as the market's expectation;\n   267\t  4. **deciding_gap:** an unresolved overlap, a change of scope or an unsized adjustment decides its band.\n   268\t- **Agreement:** where the two readers agree, that is the reading. Where they disagree, a third fresh reader (Codex) reads the card with\n   269\t  both readings and the passages, and its answer decides. No reading is changed after the verdict is computed.\n   270\t- **Verdict** (`paid_gate.py verdict`, D725's lines, on the first attempts; computed once and sealed, §3f):\n   271\t  - **PASS:** the joint arm has both cards usable in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot, and at least 3 more usable\n   272\t    pairs than the separate arm; and zero published defects (contradiction, internal reference or deciding gap) across the joint and\n   273\t    singleton answers.\n   274\t  - **STOP:** any such published defect; a coverage line missed; a gain under 3.\n   275\t  - **INCOMPLETE:** a packet or a card missing a reading, or a unit not sent.\n   276\t  - This is a small design test, not release acceptance.\n   277\t\n   278\t## 5. What the result decides\n   279\t\n   280\t- **PASS:** judging-v13 earns a production design: automatic families in matching, lifecycle and the request builder, each a further\n   281\t  step Alex opens.\n   282\t- **STOP or INCOMPLETE:** the line goes back to Alex with the readings, and nothing is tuned on these 36 answers.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
