ind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U01.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U02.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U02.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U02.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U02.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U02.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U03.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U03.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U03.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U03.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U03.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U04.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U04.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U04.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U04.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U04.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U06.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U06.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U06.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U06.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U06.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U07.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U07.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U07.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U07.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U07.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U08.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U08.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U08.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U08.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U08.md\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U09.claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U09.codex-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U09.codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U09.codex.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U09.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The D745 targeted test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745] and scored by\n     4\t`blind4.py tally` (`TALLY.json`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL (registered line \"every S and E reply has a settled yes\")\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Case | Writer | Shape | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". All four readers, both families, said\n    44\t    not supported.\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 4. What the paid re-send would meet\n    65\t\n    66\tUnder the paid gate's lines (`PAID-GATE.md` §4), a published singleton with a \"deciding gap\" stops the run. A deciding gap includes \"an\n    67\tunsized adjustment decides its band\". Where the two readers disagree, the third reader is Codex. On blind4's evidence, a published S\n    68\tor E forecast built like these replies would very likely be marked by Codex, and the run would stop. An S or E answer held as\n    69\tunresolved is not published and cannot stop it.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 5. Not done\n    72\t\n    73\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. No request text changed after the registration.\n     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, third run after D742: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D742])\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP on one registered line, as written** (council round 57 confirms): \"an accepted reply with a named defect (marked by both readers …)\".\n     4\t- **R01 (S, Claude writer).** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on F14: the outcome is a range of $679.25–745B, and rung 4\n     5\t  ($700–800B) does not hold all of it.\n     6\t- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n     7\t  with the reason in reasons\". The answer gives that reason: \"the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4\". The code (S6) checks\n     8\t  overlap.\n     9\t- **The cause is my reader brief.** Its definition of broken structure reads \"a rung whose band does not hold the outcome\", without the\n    10\t  schema's range clause. Both readers applied the brief as written. No earlier run had a range that crossed a rung edge, so the gap was\n    11\t  not exercised before.\n    12\t\n    13\tEverything else in the run is clean:\n    14\t- **Writers:** 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, so there was no refusal review.\n    15\t- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n    16\t  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n    17\t- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n    18\t- **The D742 rule was followed by every P-family writer.** All six recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark, then\n    19\t  computed Q4 from it. None computed a guidance rate straight into the calculation.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    22\t`schema.json`. The tally is in `TALLY.json`.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    25\t\n    26\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| P (joint) | v4 416.3, v8 386.1, both rung 2 | v4 420.9, v8 391.0, both rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v4 | 419.3, rung 2 | 416.3, rung 2 |\n    30\t| P-v8 | 387.4, rung 2 | 388.1, rung 2 |\n    31\t| S (CY2026) | 679.25–745, rung 4 | 670.5, rung 3 |\n    32\t| O | 9–20, rung 1 | 16, rung 1 |\n    33\t| E | 37, rung 3 | 34, rung 3 |\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    36\t\n    37\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    38\t- R01 broken structure (both readers' marks), explained above;\n    39\t- R01 \"two forecasts\" (Microsoft backed out of the four-company tallies, a reader matter through `rests_on`);\n    40\t- two \"plus or minus 2%\" midpoint marks (R02, R09) and a rounding difference within tolerance (R08). In all, both adjudicators\n    41\t  rejected 8 marks covering 7 distinct allegations (F14 was marked twice);\n    42\t- R06's \"number misread\" claims on the $18B schedule and the Lancium tranche.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Support:**\n    45\t- **R07 (S, Codex writer) is settled \"not supported\".** Its rung rests on a 90% data-center share, while the passages put the share near\n    46\t  100%:\n    47\t  - e1947: more than $730B \"on AI infrastructure\" against $720–745B of capex;\n    48\t  - e2759 and e5376: NVIDIA's \"nearly $800 billion\" for the top five, written both as capex and as data-center capex;\n    49\t  - Meta's own figures: about 96%.\n    50\t  At 94% or more, the same $745B gives rung 4. The reply is accepted and publishable, but its rung does not follow from the evidence.\n    51\t  This is not a registered stop line (usable is counted per case, and S has R01). It is a real judgement error in an answer that would\n    52\t  publish.\n    53\t- **R06 (O, Claude writer) is split again** on the $18B reading: Codex says not supported, Claude says supported. This is the same\n    54\t  passage as in both earlier runs.\n    55\t- Every other forecast is supported by both readers or both adjudicators.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. What the test shows\n    58\t\n    59\t- **The writer-side contract holds.** In three runs, the request has moved from 11 of 12 accepted with a checker error (run 1), to 11 of\n    60\t  12 with a request contradiction (run 2), to 12 of 12 with no refusal (run 3). D742's rule was followed by all six writers it applies\n    61\t  to.\n    62\t- **The stop this time is in the measuring instrument.** The reader brief's definition is narrower than the format the writer was\n    63\t  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n    64\t- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n    65\t  kind of error the paid re-send is meant to measure in gpt-6-luna.\n    66\t- **Council round 57 found two more points** (`codex-r57.md`):\n    67\t  - R01, the only usable S reply, marks Zacks's $720–745B of *total* capex as the market reference for *data-center* capex. Claude's\n    68\t    reader noticed it in its ground but filed no named defect.\n    69\t  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n    70\t    sits in rung 2.\n    71\t- **Open disclosures:**\n    72\t  - O's $18B reading (split three runs running);\n    73\t  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n    74\t  - R07's unsupported share.\n    75\t\n    76\t## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n    77\t\n    78\tFresh readers re-read the same 12 replies under the corrected range rule. Two controls were mixed in, under a fresh shuffle Q01–Q14.\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The range repair works.**\n    81\t  - C-disjoint was marked broken structure by both readers.\n    82\t  - C-overlap was marked by neither.\n    83\t  - R01 (now Q07), the range across a rung edge, was not marked broken structure by either reader.\n    84\t- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\n    85\t  - Both readers marked \"number misread\" on F6: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as its midpoint, 108.\n    86\t  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n    87\t    or its midpoint as value\".\n    88\t  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n    89\t- **The class.** The readers' packet carries the user text and the answer, but not the format rules the writer was given. The\n    90\t  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n    91\t  (the range, the midpoint) come from this one gap.\n    92\t- **C-overlap's other mark is my error.** Both readers marked \"two forecasts\" on C-overlap because my control kept E's original text\n    93\t  (\"85% … $37 billion\") beside the new range. The readers were right to flag the inconsistency. It does not touch the range rule the\n    94\t  control tests.\n    95\t- **Judgement noise:**\n    96\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n    97\t    readers now.\n    98\t  - S's Claude reply (Q07) is split at both levels this time: Codex says not supported and upholds two marks; Claude rejects them.\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   101\t\n   102\t## 5. The second blinded re-read (D744, Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; `reread2-packets/`, `reread2-adjudication/`, `REREAD2-TALLY.json`)\n   103\t\n   104\tReaders now get the same rule text as adjudicators (`blind_rules.py`). There were five clean controls, under a fresh shuffle T01–T17.\n   105\t\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
