{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10957,10994p;11346,11378p;11416,11543p'\"","aggregated_output":" 10957\t## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n 10958\t\n 10959\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-22): \"work with counsel and have a look at the traces of this problem … what is wrong with the system design\n 10960\t  overall … look at the code, look at the traces, deeply battle it out with counsel … because I think it's the most important thing\n 10961\t  that we don't hallucinate with the numbers, and I want you to get this right\".\n 10962\t- **The record:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/` (`RESULT.md`).\n 10963\t  - Claude's reflection was written before Codex's.\n 10964\t  - Codex ran an independent investigation (fresh thread `01a20501…`) and a challenge round.\n 10965\t  - Consensus. One wording point stays open: the $146B is arithmetically a residual, but the order of the judge's choices is unknown.\n 10966\t- **Diagnosis:**\n 10967\t  1. Answers are prose; code ties no figure to a source and recomputes nothing. The production check passes contradictory or invented\n 10968\t     figures.\n 10969\t  2. Related questions compute the same quantities separately. The unit is one question, with 0 dependencies.\n 10970\t  3. Filing and the evidence in view split their inputs. The previous conclusion carries figures without provenance.\n 10971\t  4. Opening's bands amplify an error.\n 10972\t- **Measured:**\n 10973\t  - Data Center Q4 above company Q4 on at least 13 of 26 days in old-1;\n 10974\t  - Data Center's full year above the total's on 20 consecutive days in old-2;\n 10975\t  - different Q3 bases on 17 of 18 paired days;\n 10976\t  - shared evidence between total and Data Center 8% in old-2;\n 10977\t  - 17 of the 24 double-rejected answers from unchecked arithmetic.\n 10978\t- **Recommended design** (a new judge version, Alex's to open):\n 10979\t  - one judging call per related family, each question keeping its answer from one calculation;\n 10980\t  - figures labelled by the judge (source, calculation, assumption; scope and period);\n 10981\t  - code checks only identity and arithmetic (figure in quote, recompute, part within whole, one value per shared quantity, a sourced\n 10982\t    reference);\n 10983\t  - unreconciled forecasts shown as unresolved;\n 10984\t  - extraction unchanged;\n 10985\t  - families declared for the test.\n 10986\t- **Interim for a first release:** number questions shown as \"Forecast unavailable\" until they pass the checks.\n 10987\t- **The test:** a $0 gate (hand reconstructions of five stored cases, four gold controls, three planted errors), then a paid gate\n 10988\t  (36 calls; joint against separate on the three draws' 09-20 pair, plus O, S and E singletons). Pass and stop lines are in `RESULT.md`.\n 10989\t- **Not done:** nothing built, sent or changed.\n 10990\t\n 10991\t## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n 10992\t\n 10993\t- **The questions** (after D725):\n 10994\t  - \"要不要开一个新的判官版本来试修法（相关的题合在一次里算，每个数字标明出处，程序检查算术和出处）？\" Options: 开，先做 0 元那一关\n 11346\t## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n 11347\t\n 11348\t- **The questions** (after D740), each with its options; the recommended option is listed first:\n 11349\t  - 【增速参考】\"写手用公司指引的三季度 1080 亿算出“指引隐含增速约 12%”，参考它定四季度增速 13%，再套在自己预测的三季度 1136 亿上。进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个。这算不算“同一季度用了两个数”的错？\" Options: 不算错 (推荐); 算错.\n 11350\t  - 【市场参照】\"分析师预期公司全年总收入 4110 亿，最近一个季度数据中心占 92.5%，乘出来约 3800 亿。能不能把这 3800 亿叫作“市场对数据中心全年的预期”？\" Options: 不能 (推荐); 能.\n 11351\t  - 【总开支当数据中心开支】\"问题问四大云厂商“数据中心资本开支”，原文只给了“总资本开支”（并说大部分投向 AI 基础设施）。一份答案直接拿总开支当数据中心开支，没扣非数据中心的部分。可以吗？\" Options: 必须写明扣多少 (推荐); 可以直接用.\n 11352\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"不算错 (推荐)\"; \"不能 (推荐)\"; \"必须写明扣多少 (推荐)\".\n 11353\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11354\t  1. **Not a defect:** a growth rate computed from guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate, when one figure\n 11355\t     supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation.\n 11356\t  2. **Not the market's reference:** consensus for a total × a past quarter's reported share. That figure is our comparison estimate.\n 11357\t     Only an outside figure for the member's own quantity (for example Morningstar's direct segment estimate), or a calculation that\n 11358\t     carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period, is the market's reference.\n 11359\t  3. **A total used for a narrower quantity** (total capex for data-center capex) must state the deduction as its own figure, or give\n 11360\t     a supported bound that shows where the narrower quantity lands.\n 11361\t- **How they are applied (D738):**\n 11362\t  - Ruling 2's carried share is structural, so S5 refuses it.\n 11363\t  - Rulings 1 and 3 go in the request and in the reader brief, because they turn on what a label or an estimate means.\n 11364\t  - Then three steps:\n 11365\t    1. fix the S1 \"from … to …\" range (D740);\n 11366\t    2. check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\n 11367\t    3. re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers.\n 11368\t\n 11369\t## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\n 11370\t\n 11371\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md`, run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/`):\n 11372\t  - **STOP on one confirmed registered line.** S4 refused P-v4's Claude writer, whose Q4 = apply_growth(own Q3 $112.5B, growth(Q2\n 11373\t    $96.2B, Q3 guidance $108B)).\n 11374\t    - Both refusal reviewers marked the condition stated and the answer right.\n 11375\t    - Both readers and both adjudicators called the forecast supported.\n 11376\t  - **Every other line passes:**\n 11377\t    - 12 of 12 schema-valid, 11 of 12 accepted;\n 11378\t    - every case usable;\n 11416\t## D743 · 2026-11-22 · The third v13s blind run stops on the reader brief; the writer side is clean; council 57 asks Alex to authorize a re-reading\n 11417\t\n 11418\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md`, run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`):\n 11419\t  - **Writers:**\n 11420\t    - 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals;\n 11421\t    - all six P-family writers recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark (D742's rule);\n 11422\t    - every case usable; the joint case supported in one reply.\n 11423\t  - **STOP as written.** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on R01 (S, Claude writer): a $679.25–745B range with rung 4 chosen and the\n 11424\t    reason given. Both adjudicators rejected it under the schema's \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons\";\n 11425\t    S6 checks overlap.\n 11426\t  - **The cause is the reader brief.** Its definition lacks the schema's range clause. This is Claude's error, and the first time a range\n 11427\t    crossed a rung edge.\n 11428\t  - **Adjudication:** both adjudicators rejected all 8 disputed marks (7 allegations); no defect upheld.\n 11429\t- **Disclosures:**\n 11430\t  - R07 (S, Codex writer) is accepted but settled \"not supported\": its 90% data-center share decides rung 3 without passage support.\n 11431\t  - R06's $18B reading is split again.\n 11432\t  - R01 marks total capex as the data-center reference.\n 11433\t  - R11 has a prose example that misses its edge.\n 11434\t- **Council round 57** (`codex-r57.md`):\n 11435\t  - It confirmed the STOP.\n 11436\t  - It recommended option (a), with Alex's authorization:\n 11437\t    - put the schema's exact range rule in both reading instructions;\n 11438\t    - have fresh readers and adjudicators re-read all 12 original replies under reshuffled ids, with no access to prior votes or the key;\n 11439\t    - add blinded controls (an explained overlap must pass; a disjoint band must be marked);\n 11440\t    - ask the readers to judge R01's substance, including its reference.\n 11441\t  - Falsifier: the corrected reading rejects the overlap control or accepts the disjoint one, or the re-read hits any registered stop\n 11442\t    (including S losing its only usable reply).\n 11443\t  - Option (b), a fourth writer run, would re-measure writer variance. Option (c) is the approval step itself.\n 11444\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22) to the question \"下一步怎么走？\": \"补上那条，重读一遍 (推荐)\". The other options were \"再完整跑一次盲测\" and\n 11445\t  \"这次就算过\".\n 11446\t- **Decided (Alex):** correct the reader brief to the schema's range rule and re-read the same 12 replies blind, with the two controls.\n 11447\t  A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send. Nothing paid before that.\n 11448\t\n 11449\t## D744 · 2026-11-22 · The D743 re-read fixes the range rule but stops on the midpoint; one mechanism (readers lack the writer's format rules); council 58 asks Alex for one more bounded re-read under shared rule text\n 11450\t\n 11451\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §4; `REREAD-TALLY.json`):\n 11452\t  - The range repair works on both controls, and R01/Q07 is no longer marked.\n 11453\t  - **STOP as written on Q10** (P-v8, Codex writer). Both readers marked \"number misread\" on \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as\n 11454\t    its midpoint. Both adjudicators rejected it under the schema rule. The same mark has appeared 11 times across four readings, and\n 11455\t    every adjudication rejected it.\n 11456\t  - No settled defect. Q07 (S, Claude writer) is split at both levels, and Q09 (O, Claude writer) is split again.\n 11457\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) is \"supported\" by both fresh readers after being \"not supported\" by both first adjudicators: reader\n 11458\t    judgement noise on a decisive assumption.\n 11459\t- **The mechanism.** Readers get the passages and the answer but not the writer's format rules, while adjudicators get both. Both stops\n 11460\t  caused by the instrument come from that gap.\n 11461\t  - C-overlap's shared \"two forecasts\" mark is Claude's control text: it kept the original \"85% / $37 billion\" beside the new range.\n 11462\t  - C-disjoint kept prose naming the right band.\n 11463\t- **Council round 58** (`codex-r58.md`):\n 11464\t  - It confirmed the STOP.\n 11465\t  - **Structural rule:** readers get the same rule text as adjudicators, from one shared source: the writer's system text, every schema\n 11466\t    description, and the defect definitions. They check quote and calculation against the format, and separately name the evidence for\n 11467\t    the assumption that sets the band.\n 11468\t  - **Controls, rebuilt clean:**\n 11469\t    - a permitted midpoint must pass;\n 11470\t    - 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\" must be marked;\n 11471\t    - a clean overlap must pass;\n 11472\t    - a clean disjoint band must be marked;\n 11473\t    - a format-correct forecast with an unsupported decisive assumption must be flagged.\n 11474\t  - The control adjudication path must resolve control answers.\n 11475\t  - Recommendation: take the STOP and the spending disagreement to Alex, and ask him to authorize one bounded blinded re-read of the\n 11476\t    same 12 replies. A fourth writer run would change the answers while the open question is the reading.\n 11477\t  - Falsifier: any failed control or any registered stop ends the re-read. Both spending replies' votes and reasons are reported beside\n 11478\t    the verdict.\n 11479\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22) to \"要不要批准再重读一次？\": \"批准再重读一次 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不再重读，直接议花钱重发\" and\n 11480\t  \"先停在这里\".\n 11481\t- **Decided (Alex):** one bounded blinded re-read under the shared rule text, with the five controls. Any failed control or registered\n 11482\t  stop ends it. A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n 11483\t\n 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11504\t     mutant.\n 11505\t  2. Add one clarification to the existing instruction: when a deduction or cut decides the band, show the evidence for its size or a\n 11506\t     bound the passages support that justifies the band; if neither, answer unresolved.\n 11507\t  3. Test on S and on one stored case where the passages do support a narrower figure.\n 11508\t  4. Before the test, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11517\t  - Rung-deciding shares, deductions and discounts must show their passage support or a justifying bound, else unresolved.\n 11518\t  - The default discount is made subject to that rule.\n 11519\t  - The three controls are the saved E 15% cut, a vague P-v8 unresolved answer and a justified S unresolved answer (expected no, no,\n 11520\t    yes).\n 11521\t  - Pass: every S and E reply settled yes on a forecast or its reason; both P-v8 replies settled yes on a forecast. A support split\n 11522\t    is inconclusive.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in E/system.txt S/system.txt E/user.txt S/user.txt; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in E/system.txt S/system.txt E/user.txt S/user.txt; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"E/system.txt\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\nS/system.txt\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\nE/user.txt\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.S/user.txt\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    13\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    14\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    15\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    16\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1174, e1446, e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    18\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n    19\t- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n    20\t    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n    21\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    22\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    23\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    24\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    25\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    33\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n    34\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    35\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n    36\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    48\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n    49\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e175; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    50\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    51\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    52\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n    53\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n    54\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n    55\t    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n    56\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    57\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    58\t    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    59\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n    60\t    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n    61\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    62\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n    63\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n    64\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n    65\t    Quote: Elon Musk said that the global shortage of memory is \"the limiting factor\" to the AI build-out\n    66\t- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n    67\t    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n    68\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1649; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    69\t- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n    70\t    Quote: Many technology companies estimate the useful life of Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) at six years.\n    71\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n    72\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n    73\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n    74\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n    75\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    76\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e4790 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c798576399fa62f56cf3145 2026-09-11] Alphabet increased its expected 2026 capital expenditures to $195 billion–$205 billion.\n    79\t    Quote: Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\n    80\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    87\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    90\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n    95\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    96\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    97\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n    98\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n    99\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   106\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   115\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   116\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   118\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   120\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   125\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   126\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
