{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files | rg '(calc_small|blind4|blind_rules)\\\\.py\"'$'\"'; rg -n 'q04|q19' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json; tail -n 7 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; rg -n 'D745|blind4' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/DECISIONS.md | tail -12\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py\n34:    \"q19\",\n199:   \"question_id\": \"q04\",\n852:   \"question_id\": \"q19\",\n| 35 | 11-05 | 独立读者挑战\"现在做进生产、跑 14 天三遍\"的建议 | 核实属实的：资本开支那一行在新句子下 12 次读漏 3 次（旧句子 9/9，模型两次都怪\"更大的行\"半句）；句子照 5 月财报改、又只在 5 月财报上量，没有留出的财报；做进生产前的记录写法（同一个数挂几道题、指引不该写成\"事实\"、第二个提示词）要 Alex 定；分类和判断同时跑、说明里还引着旧句子，照实记下 | 建议撤回；下一步：修掉资本开支漏读（改成\"写完逐条复核\"），在 5 月和留出的 8 月两份财报上各测一遍，约 0.5 美元 |\n| 36 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好开始做到production 上，完整的更新所有系统设计等\"；同一个数挂几道题，他选\"存一条，挂到每道读到它的题\"（D364）。做进生产前先修资本开支漏读：第四版把\"是不是这道题的量\"挪到最后逐条复核（并删掉别的期间），文件规则补\"有申报的只读申报的\"；Codex 审 3 轮（共 6 条严重，都改）；试发 7 道题过；然后 5 月登记读法、8 月留出读法 | **没过**：5 月\"不能删掉该留的\"——v22 只留住 1/3、资本开支 3/6 读；8 月资本开支二季度 0/3（选了上半年、引用跳格被拒）。机理：复核里\"别的期间删掉\"让全年题删掉最近一季；\"最近一期\"在本季和年初至今两栏间没说清。好消息：8 月 v9 找到了新披露的担保 1,085 亿和承诺 560 亿 | 停，交 Alex：第三版是唯一过了 5 月三条的版本 |\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task: Alex chooses whether to authorize a targeted $0 test (D745).** The test covers:\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:465:**Earlier next task (done, D745): Alex chooses whether to authorize one more bounded blinded re-read (D744).**\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1089:| D745 | 2026-11-23 | D744 re-read: readers calibrated (5/5 controls, no shared false mark); STOP on a qualifier-list gap (\"an estimated\" forced to none) and on S zero usable (unsupported data-center share decides the band; E's 15% cut the same); council 59: targeted $0 test of one clarification (show evidence or a supported bound for a band-deciding deduction, else unresolved) plus the qualifier fix; Alex: \"批准针对性小测\", and a justified unresolved answer counts as usable (\"算合格\") | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in reader-packets/U0?.{claude,codex}.json adjudication/U0?.{claude,codex}.json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in reader-packets/U0?.{claude,codex}.json adjudication/U0?.{claude,codex}.json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"reader-packets/U01.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"e109 and e2650 (reported H1 FY2027 Data Center revenue $75.25bn + $89.0bn = $164.25bn, F3); e2652 (Q3 guidance $108.0bn plus or minus 2%); e5054, e5055 (beat history behind the 5% beat, F11); e5005 (12% sequential pace benchmark for Q4, F13); e3406, e5061 (hyperscale reaccelerates in Q4); e3399 (Data Center mix 92.5% and rising, F16); e3733, e5067 (supply about 70% of demand, constrained through FY2028); e2295 (Morningstar FY2027 Data Center $361bn); e4333 (FY2027 total revenue consensus $411bn); e1135 (SemiAnalysis H2 Data Center +20% over estimates)\", \"why_not\": \"The stated gap ('The outlook for the second half is uncertain') is generic uncertainty, not a gap the passages leave open, and the writer's own figures settle the rung. With reported H1 Data Center revenue of $164.25bn (e109, e2650), rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn) holds for any H2 Data Center revenue from $185.75bn to below $235.75bn. The writer's supported H2 build is $223.1bn: Q3 guidance $108bn (e2652) plus a 5% beat (e5054, e5055), a 12% Q4 pace benchmarked on e5005, and a 92.8% mix edging up from 92.5% (e3399). That gives $387.35bn (F18), inside rung 2. Morningstar's $361bn (e2295) and the $411bn consensus at a ~92.5% mix (~$380bn) also sit in rung 2. Rung 3 would need Q4 Data Center revenue below about $86-88bn, a drop from Q2's $89bn and Q3's ~$98-105bn, against management's Q4 reacceleration (e3406, e5061). Rung 1 would need roughly $130bn or more in Q4, about 24% sequential growth on top of a Q3 beat, against supply covering about 70% of demand (e3733, e5067). Only SemiAnalysis's '+20% over analyst estimates' (e1135), measured against an unstated pre-Q2 base, points higher, and the answer does not name it as the gap. The evidence supports a rung 2 forecast; the system text says 'not established' and uncertainty alone are not reasons to withhold one.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U02.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Rung 4 ($700-800bn) is bounded by passages: four-company 2026 capex tallies of $720-745bn (e5014), near $745bn (e1856) and $760bn (e2458); the four companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending of more than $730bn (e1947, F12), a floor inside rung 4 that sits at or above the total-capex tally and so supports a data-center share near 100% (F14); and the five-company figures of about $795-800bn (e2759, e5055, with e4766/e5376 calling that capex data-center spending), which keep the four below $800bn. The build: guided Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn (e1549) and Amazon $220bn (e1649) sum to $540-570bn (F4); Microsoft $165bn (F10, own estimate from the e922 run-rate path of about $156bn and the tally-implied $150-205bn, F9); total $720bn midpoint (F11) x 98% share (F14) = $705.6bn (F15). The point sits only $5.6bn above the rung-3 edge and depends on two own estimates (the $165bn above the run-rate path, and the 2% residual, which no passage sizes; the stated 96% lower share bound or the low guidance end would give about $691bn), so rung 4 rests on the passage bounds above rather than on the precision of the point.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}},\n     2\t \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U03.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F4, the writer's own 85% 'realization share' of the $20 billion FY2027 CPU guidance (F1, e3660), rests on F1, e4689 and e5044. It gives F5 = $17 billion. F6, an exact 2x multiple benchmarked on e4691/e4856, then gives F7 = $34 billion, which falls in rung 3. The 15% haircut in F4 is what decides the band: without it the guidance path gives 20 x 2 = $40 billion, the rung-2 lower edge, and 'more than double' points above that.\", \"why_not\": \"Nothing in the passages gives the size of the haircut or a bound for it. e3660 says NVIDIA 'still expects' $20 billion, a reaffirmation. e4689 (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating) point to broad uptake, so they argue against a shortfall, not for one. The AMD benchmark claims (e5400, e5403) are a competitor's own tests and say nothing about Vera sales; the answer itself calls their effect 'uncertain'. No passage reports a delay, a supply constraint, a cut or lower volumes. The answer gives no time-and-execution reasoning (selling quarters left, implied volume or price) that would justify a discount. 'No committed purchase volumes' is missing confirmation, not evidence that revenue will come in 15% below guidance. The passages also treat the $20 billion as Vera CPU revenue (e4691 'Vera CPU revenue to double'; e4856), so the split between the 'CPU opportunity' and 'realized Vera revenue' has no source. The multiple is also set at exactly 2 although e4856/e4764 say 'more than double'. Because the reference sits exactly on the $40 billion boundary, any unsupported haircut moves the answer to rung 3, so the rung rests entirely on an assumption the passages do not support.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U04.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"Unresolved on the claim that no passage gives the four companies' data-center share of total capex: total capex is $720-760bn (e5014, e1856, e2458), so about 94% is the share at which spending reaches $700bn. The answer treats e1947 (AI infrastructure, more than $730bn) as a broader category and e5376 (nearly $800bn) as a five-company figure. Its own working estimate of a 90% share (F12, giving F13 = 670.5) has no passage behind it.\", \"why_not\": \"The gap does not keep the rung open. e1947 says these same four companies will spend more than $730bn on AI infrastructure in 2026. AI infrastructure is spending inside the data-center category, not wider than it, so this passage sets a floor above $700bn. The ceiling is the four companies' total capex of $720-760bn (e5014, e1856, e2458), which is below $800bn. That puts the outcome in rung 4. The answer calls AI infrastructure 'broader' but cites nothing for that. The answer's own share evidence points the same way: Meta's $130bn AI capex (e3686) against its $125-145bn total guidance (e1549) is about 96%, above the 94% line the answer names. e4082 (AI data-center infrastructure about $750bn) and e1416 describe the same spending as data-center spending. Nothing in the passages points to non-data-center capex large enough to bring the share below about 94%. The rung is the most likely outcome, not a proven one, and the passages make rung 4 the most likely. The answer's 90% (F12) is its own unsupported assumption, and the rung-3 figure it produces (F13) is left unused.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U05.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"Stated assumption F4: NVIDIA realizes 90% of the $40 billion reference F3 (F1 $20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue from e3660 x F2 'double' from e4691), resting on e4689, e5044 and e5403; F5 = $36 billion, rung 3.\", \"why_not\": \"The rung is decided entirely by F4's 10% shortfall. The reference sits exactly on the $40 billion boundary between rungs 2 and 3, and management's own statements (e3660, e4764, e4856) say CPU revenue will 'more than double', which puts the guidance at or above $40 billion. Any discount crosses the boundary, so the size of the discount decides the band. No passage sizes a shortfall or bounds the outcome below $40 billion: there is no delay, cancellation, supply constraint, quantified revision, order size or shipment pace, and the answer itself says so ('The passages give no order sizes or shipment pace'). Of the three passages F4 rests on, e4689 (broad deployment) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating Vera) point upward, and the answer marks both toward_first. The third, e5403, is AMD's own per-core benchmark claim, which the answer admits does not quantify lost sales. So the 90% is the writer's unsized judgement, not a size or bound drawn from the passages, and the move from the reference's rung 2 to rung 3 does not follow from the evidence shown.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U06.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"The band is decided by the growth pace F5 (1.8x on the F1 $20 billion FY2027 CPU base from e3660). The passages bracket that pace: the server-CPU market pace of about 1.5x (e4687; e4857's fivefold 2026-2030 is about 48% a year) is the lower anchor, and the reaffirmed 'more than double' floor (e3660, e4764) is the upper end. The discount below the floor follows the system rule for a target whose volume is not yet shown: deployment is described in the future tense (e4689) and Anthropic is only evaluating Vera (e5044). AMD's own benchmark claims (e5399-e5404) are a light signal. e4691 and e4856 attribute the doubling to Vera, which supports a near-full Vera share. At that share, every pace in the bracket gives $30-40 billion, which is rung 3. The unsized 90% Vera share (F7) does not decide the band, since rung 3 holds for any share from about 84% to 100% at 1.8x (30/36). The exact 1.8x and 90% are the writer's judgements; the bracket and the sensitivity are what justify the rung.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U07.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 FY2027 Data Center reported $164.27bn (e109 $75.25bn + e4985 $89.02bn; matches e5309 $164bn). Q3: company revenue estimated at $112bn, just above the $108bn +/-2% guidance (e2652) on the Q2 beat (e3200) and the outside beat-trend estimate of $113.6bn (e5056), times the 92.5% Data Center share reported for Q2 (e3399) = $103.6bn. Q4: 13% sequential growth against the 12% Q3 guided benchmark (e5314) on management's stated Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (e4283, e5061), limited by supply constraints (e5067, e4771, e5205), at a 93% share (share rose every quarter per e3399) = $117.7bn. Sum $385.6bn, in rung 2. The rung does not hinge on the 13% pace: flat Q4 company revenue at 92.5% still gives about $371bn, falling below $350bn would need second-half Data Center revenue under $185.7bn (a sharp decline nothing in the passages indicates), and reaching $400bn would need Q4 Data Center revenue of about $132bn (roughly 27% sequential company growth), which the stated supply constraints (e5067, e3733) bound out.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U08.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F13 = F11 total capex $745bn minus F12 $25bn spending outside data centers = $720bn, in rung 4 [700, 800). The $745bn total rests on the four-company tallies: e5014 ($720-745bn, top end), e1856 (near $745bn), e2458 ($760bn) and e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure). The band is decided by the deduction, which must stay below about $45bn at a $745bn total. The passages bound it as small. e1947's more than $730bn of AI infrastructure for the same four companies sits close to the total. Meta's AI capex of $130bn (e3686) against its $125-145bn guidance (e1549, midpoint $135bn) implies about 4% outside AI, roughly $28bn on $745bn. Figures labelled as data-center spending, e4082 and e1416 (about $750bn), also put it above $700bn. The margin is thin: at the Zacks low of $720bn, a Meta-like deduction lands near $693bn. Still, e1947's floor of more than $730bn on AI infrastructure supports rung 4.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U09.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 Data Center revenue is reported: e109 ($75.25bn) + e2650 ($89.0bn) = $164.25bn (F11, matching e5309's $164bn). H2 comes from the Q3 company guidance of $108.0bn +/-2% (e2652) raised 4.5% (F4) on the guidance-beat record (e5054, e5055, e3200, e2452), giving $112.86bn. Q4 is set at +12% sequential (F6), using the guide-implied pace as the benchmark (e4899, e5005) and held because of the expected Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (e3406, e5061) and supply limits (e3733, e5067, e3115). That gives $126.40bn. The Q2 Data Center share of 92.5% (e3399, e5116, e5032) is held flat for H2 (F9). FY2027 Data Center revenue = $385.57bn (F12), inside rung 2 ($350-400bn). The band holds as a bound. Rung 1 needs H2 Data Center revenue of at least about $235.75bn: at a 92.5% share that means Q4 growth of roughly 25% sequential, faster than any recent quarter (Q2 +18%, Q3 guided +12%), with supply capped at about 70% of demand. A share of about 98.5% would also get there, and that is implausible. Even 20% Q4 growth gives about $394bn. Rung 3 would need H2 company revenue to fall to about $201bn, i.e. Q4 below the Q3 guide, which the reacceleration guidance contradicts. The consensus cross-checks also fall in rung 2: $411bn company (e4333) and the Morningstar $361bn reference (e2295).\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/U01.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v8\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"e109 and e2650 establish $164.25 billion of first-half Data Center revenue. NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint (e2652), the roughly 92.5% recent Data Center share (e3399), and the implied $112.2 billion Q4 company revenue (e2452, e5162) yield about $368 billion for the year at a steady share. Morningstar's direct $361 billion segment forecast (e2295) and the answer's own $387.35 billion estimate also land in rung 2.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"The answer cites general second-half uncertainty without identifying a gap that prevents a forecast. Q4 shipments and the segment share remain uncertain, but the evidence supports $350 billion to below $400 billion as the most likely band.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U02.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"The answer applies an estimated 98% data-center share to $720 billion of total capex, yielding $705.6 billion in rung 4. The share rests on e1947, e5014, e3686, e1549, e1649, e1416 and e4082. The total includes a $165 billion Microsoft estimate based on e922, e4405 and the implied remainder from e5014.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"At $720 billion of total capex, rung 4 requires a data-center share of at least 97.2%. The passages do not establish that share. e1947 covers broader AI infrastructure and does not establish a data-center spending floor. e5014 says only that a large portion of total capex goes to AI infrastructure. Meta's AI spending and Amazon's memory-driven increase do not establish the four-company mix, and the $750 billion estimates do not specify the same four firms. Even the answer's claimed 96% lower bound would yield $691.2 billion, in rung 3. Microsoft's reported quarter and Azure backlog also do not establish the assumed sequential spending pace.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U03.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v41\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"F7 forecasts $34 billion by doubling a $17 billion FY2027 Vera estimate. The $17 billion comes from assuming Vera realizes 85% of e3660's $20 billion CPU outlook. e4691 supports doubling as a benchmark, while e4689 and e5044 are cited for the 85% assumption.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"Expected deployments in e4689 and Anthropic's evaluation in e5044 provide no purchase volumes, pricing, or Vera share of CPU revenue. AMD's benchmarks in e5400 and e5403 do not quantify the effect on sales. Even at exactly double, rung 3 requires Vera to realize at least 75% and less than 100% of the $20 billion outlook. The passages do not establish those bounds.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U04.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"e5014, e1856 and e2458 estimate total capex for the four firms. e1947 puts their broader AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion, while e5376 puts data-center capex for five firms near $800 billion without sizing the fifth firm's contribution. The roughly $750 billion estimates in e1416 and e4082 do not identify the four firms as their scope. At $745 billion of total capex, the four firms would need to spend about 94% of it on data centers to reach $700 billion. The passages do not establish whether their combined share is above or below that threshold, and the 90% assumption in F12 has no quantified support for the four firms together.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U05.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v41\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"The forecast applies F4's assumed 90% realization rate to the $40 billion management reference derived from e3660 and e4691. F4 cites broad deployment plans (e4689), Anthropic's evaluation (e5044), and AMD's competitive benchmarks (e5403).\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"Rung 3 requires at least 75% but less than 100% of the $40 billion reference. The deployment plans give no committed volumes or shipment timing, and the evaluation and benchmarks do not quantify delayed or lost sales. The passages support neither bound, and the reports of potentially more than doubling leave the upside unsized.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U06.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v41\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"F8 gets $32.4 billion from the $20 billion FY2027 total CPU guidance in e3660, a forecast FY2028 multiple of 1.8x in F5, and an estimated Vera share of 90% in F7. F5 draws on e3660, e4687, e4857, e4689, e4692, e5044, and e5399–e5404. F7 draws chiefly on e4691, e4856, and e4689.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"The market growth forecasts do not establish a growth floor for NVIDIA. Deployment plans, a customer evaluation, and competitor benchmarks give no shipment or revenue figures that justify the 1.8x multiple. At the resulting $36 billion total, Vera needs at least 83⅓% of CPU revenue to reach rung 3. The Vera-specific doubling statements give no dollar base for Vera, and the deployment claims give no revenue split that supports that minimum share.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U07.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v8\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"e109 and e4985 give $164.27 billion in first-half Data Center revenue. Q3 guidance, the prior guidance beat, and the outside $113.6 billion scenario (e2652, e3200, e5056) support the $112 billion company estimate. The recent 92.5% segment share and Data Center-led growth (e3399, e5223) support carrying that mix forward. At the resulting Q3 estimate, rung 2 requires Q4 Data Center revenue of $82.13 billion to below $132.13 billion. The 12% Q3 guidance benchmark, expected Q4 hyperscaler acceleration, and shipment constraints (e5314, e4283, e5067, e5205) support a Q4 pace within that interval at a share near the recent mix. The Q4 analyst growth forecast and prior-year actual (e5162, e2452) independently imply about $112 billion in Q4 company revenue, which at the recent mix would still put the full year near $372 billion, within rung 2.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U08.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"The forecast subtracts an estimated $25 billion of spending outside data centers from $745 billion of total capex, producing $720 billion in rung 4. The total draws on e5014, e1856, and e2458. The deduction rests on e1947 and a comparison of Meta's AI capex estimate in e3686 with its total capex guidance in e1549.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"At a $745 billion total, rung 4 requires spending outside data centers to be at most $45 billion. The four-company AI infrastructure tally in e1947 does not break out data-center capex, and Meta's spending mix cannot establish a bound for all four companies. The broader estimates in e1416 and e4082 do not isolate these four, and e5376 covers five without sizing the fifth company's share.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reader-packets/U09.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v8\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue was $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). Q3 guidance and recent guidance beats support the $112.86 billion company forecast (e2652, e3200, e5054–e5056). The 12% Q4 growth assumption uses the Q3 guidance pace as a benchmark, with the Rubin rollout supporting continued growth and supply constraints limiting upside (e4899, e3406, e2759, e3115, e5067). The 92.5% Data Center share is anchored by the latest quarter and the preceding upward trend (e3399, e5116). At that share and the stated Q3 forecast, Q4 company revenue can range from about $88 billion to just under $142 billion and still land in rung 2. Even the lower Q4 analyst estimate implied by e2452 and e5162 falls inside that interval. The resulting $385.57 billion forecast is supported within the $350 billion to below $400 billion band.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}adjudication/U02.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Rung 4 ($700-800bn) is set by an assumption that rests on bounds in the passages, not on the precision of the point. (1) Four-company total: every tally for exactly these four is at or above $720bn: e5014 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 'tracking near $745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion in capex in 2026', e2079 'guided roughly $720 billion ... to $745 billion'. The answer's own total, $720bn (F11 = F4 $540-570bn guided + F10 $165bn), sits at the low end of these. Its Microsoft estimate falls inside the tally-implied $150-205bn (F9), and it follows the system text's instruction to forecast a run-rate pace (16% a quarter compounds to the reported 84%, e922) rather than annualise one quarter flat. (2) Data-center share: F14 is written as its own figure, as the format requires ('write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound'). The passages put the share near 100%. e1947 names the same four companies and the same year: 'have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', which is at or above their total-capex tally. e5376 and e4766 call NVIDIA's top-five figure 'data center capital expenditures' ('nearly $800 billion'), the same figure e2759 calls 'CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers'. So the passages treat hyperscaler capex as data-center spending, and any share from 97.2% to 100% on $720bn gives rung 4. The answer also states e1947's floor directly ('the supported floor from e1947 ... also lies in rung 4'), and >$730bn is inside rung 4. e5014's 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure' does not cap the data-center share, because non-AI cloud spending in data centers is still data-center capex. (3) Ceiling: the near-$800bn figures cover five companies (e5055 lists Oracle), so the four sit below $800bn, and the highest four-company tally is $760bn. Reader 1 is right that the 2% residual has no passage behind its size, and that the answer's stated 96% lower share gives $691.2bn. But the 96% comes from Meta's AI-only capex (e3686), which is narrower than data-center capex. The rung rests on the passage bounds above, which a reader can check, so the forecast is supported.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nadjudication/U04.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The answer says no passage gives the four companies' data-center share, so it cannot tell whether spending is above or below $700bn (about 94% of total capex). That gap does not survive the strongest passages. The ceiling is firm: data-center capex cannot exceed the four companies' total capex, which is 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' (e5014), 'near $745 billion' (e1856) and '$760 billion' (e2458). All of these are below $800bn. For the floor, e1947 names the same four firms: 'Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year.' The answer calls this 'a broader AI category' but cites no passage for that, and no passage describes AI infrastructure as reaching beyond data-center spending. Every other passage points the same way. e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year', matching the four-firm totals. e5376 puts five-firm 'data center capital expenditures' at 'nearly $800 billion', against five-firm total capex of 'roughly $795 billion' (e5055), which is a share of about 100%. Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549) is about 96% at the midpoint, above the 94% line the answer names. No passage sizes non-data-center capex large enough to pull the share below about 94%. The system text says a rung is 'the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely ... not the one it has proved' and that 'not established' is 'never a reason to stay'. The answer's reason is a lack of confirmation, not a real gap: the passages make rung 4 the most likely outcome, though not a proven one. The answer's own 90% share (F12, giving F13 = 670.5) has no passage behind it and is left unused, so it does not keep the rung open.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nadjudication/U06.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The rung depends on two assumptions, and the passages bound both. (1) Pace: the guidance is 'expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028' (e3660; e4764). The system text requires that 'A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted ... taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand', and no passage shows volume: 'Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU' (e5044) and 'will be deployed by' (e4689). So taking 2x or more in full (which rung 2 needs) is excluded. The lower anchor is the market pace: 'increase at an annual rate of 50%' (e4687) and BofA's '$35 billion to $170 billion' from this year to 2030 (e4857), about 1.5x a year. That brackets the pace at 1.5x-2.0x. (2) Share: two passages call the guided revenue Vera revenue itself. e4691 says 'Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year', and e4856 calls it Vera CPU revenue that 'could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027'. So Reader 1 is wrong that the Vera statements give no dollar base, and the passages support a near-full Vera share. At a near-full share, every pace in the bracket gives $30-40 billion, which is rung 3. The writer's 90% is more conservative than the passages require, and at the stated 1.8x the outcome stays in rung 3 for any share of 83.3% or more (30/36). The arithmetic checks: 20 x 1.8 = 36 and 36 x 90% = 32.4. The rung follows from passage-based bounds, and a reader can check them. Reader 1's objection is fair that a market pace is not a hard floor for one vendor. But the format asks for a bound grounded in the passages, not a proof, and nothing in the passages points to a discount large enough to fall below the market pace.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nadjudication/U08.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Rung 4 [700, 800) holds when total capex minus spending outside data centers is at least $700bn. At F11's $745bn, that means the deduction must stay at or below about $45bn. Both inputs rest on passages. The $745bn total is within the four-company tallies: e1856 'tracking near $745 billion', e5014 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' (its top end) and e2458 '$760 billion'. Choosing the top end follows from the raised plans in e1254, e1649 and e1549. The answer writes the $25bn deduction as its own figure, as the format rule requires ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'). It sizes the deduction from two passage comparisons, as the system text asks ('show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung'). First, e1947 says the same four companies 'will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure', which leaves under about $15bn outside AI at a $745bn total. Second, Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549, midpoint $135bn) is about 4% outside AI, roughly $28bn when scaled to $745bn. Both comparisons put the deduction well below the $45bn the rung allows. Other passages labelled as data-center spending agree: e4082 ('AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion') and e5376 (five companies at 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures'). Reader 1 is right that no passage breaks out data-center capex company by company. But the definition asks only for a size or bound that rests on the passages, not a full breakdown, and these comparisons give a reader-checkable bound that justifies rung 4. The margin is thin at the low end of the Zacks range, but the forecast point and its deduction are each tied to cited passages.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nadjudication/U02.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"At $720 billion of total capex, rung 4 requires a data-center share of about 97.2%. The answer assumes 98%, but its own claimed lower bound of 96% gives $691.2 billion, in rung 3. The format requires the passages to support the share's size or give a bound that justifies the rung. e1947 says “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure,” and e5014 says “a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.” Neither passage quantifies data-center capex for the four companies. Meta's AI capex and Amazon's memory-driven spending increase do not establish the combined share. The $750 billion estimates do not identify the same four companies, and NVIDIA's nearly $800 billion data-center estimate covers five companies without breaking out the fifth. Microsoft's reported 84% year-over-year increase and Azure backlog also do not establish the assumed 16% quarterly spending growth.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}adjudication/U04.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"At $745 billion in total capex, the four companies would need to spend about 94% of it on data centers to reach $700 billion. e1947 says “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure” and leaves open how much qualifies as data-center capex. e5376 gives “nearly $800 billion” for the “big five” and leaves the fifth company's share unspecified. The roughly $750 billion estimates in e1416 and e4082 do not identify the four companies as their scope. Meta's AI capex estimate covers one company, and e5014 describes AI infrastructure only as “a large portion” of the group's total. The passages support neither F12's 90% share nor a share high enough to put the four companies in rung 4.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}adjudication/U06.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v41\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"Rung 3 requires at least $30 billion in FY2028 Vera revenue. At the forecast $36 billion in total CPU revenue, Vera must account for at least 83⅓%. The format requires the passages to support the size of a share or discount that decides the rung, or to provide a bound that justifies it. The claim that Vera will be deployed by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM” (e4689) and the expectation that Vera revenue will “double next year” (e4691) do not establish that share. The 50% growth estimate in e4687 is for the server CPU market and gives no lower bound for NVIDIA. Anthropic “is evaluating” Vera (e5044), which supports discounting the target for execution risk but gives no basis for the size of the discount. Even at the assumed 90% Vera share, the proposed 1.5x lower anchor yields $27 billion, in rung 4.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}adjudication/U08.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"At $745 billion in total capital spending, rung 4 requires spending outside data centers to be at most $45 billion. The format requires the passages to support the size of the deduction or give a bound that justifies the rung. The four-company tally in e1947 says “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure” and gives no data-center split. Meta’s $130 billion in AI capex (e3686) and $125 billion to $145 billion in total capex guidance (e1549) cover one company and give no split between data centers and other AI spending. The roughly $750 billion estimates in e1416 and e4082 do not isolate the four companies, and e5376 covers five without sizing the fifth company’s share. The passages therefore support neither the $25 billion deduction nor the $45 billion ceiling needed for rung 4.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py | tail -230'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py | tail -230'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\timport sys\n     8\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     9\t\n    10\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    11\tfrom calc_small import QUALIFIERS  # noqa: E402\n    12\t\n    13\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    14\tnot a defect):\n    15\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    16\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages (its size, or a bound that justifies the rung). For\n    17\t  an unresolved member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung\n    18\t  unsettled (a ruling).\n    19\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    20\t  format rules permit.\n    21\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    22\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    23\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    24\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    25\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    26\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    27\t  quarter.\n    28\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    29\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    30\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    31\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    32\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    33\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    34\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    35\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    39\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    40\t    found = {}\n    41\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    42\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    43\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    44\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    45\t            if key != 'description':\n    46\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    47\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    48\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    49\t        for value in schema:\n    50\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    51\t    return found\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    55\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    56\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    57\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    58\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    59\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    60\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    61\t                      'The qualifier values the format allows (a qualifier not in this list cannot be recorded): '\n    62\t                      + ', '.join(QUALIFIERS) + '.'])\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    19\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    20\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    21\timport blind_test as bt  # noqa: E402\n    22\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    23\tfrom blind_reread2 import READER_BRIEF as _BRIEF2  # noqa: E402\n    24\tfrom blind_rules import format_material  # noqa: E402\n    25\tfrom hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\n    26\t\n    27\tCASES = ('S', 'E', 'P-v8')\n    28\t_STEP2 = '2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.'\n    29\tif _BRIEF2.count(_STEP2) != 1:\n    30\t    raise SystemExit('the second re-read brief no longer holds its step 2')\n    31\tREADER_BRIEF = _BRIEF2.replace('second re-read, D744', 'targeted test, D745').replace(_STEP2, (\n    32\t    '2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\\n'\n    33\t    '   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that remains\\n'\n    34\t    '   after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.'))\n    35\tBASE = HERE.parents[2] / 'poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\n    36\tPROMPT = \"\"\"You are a writer in a blind test of a forecasting request. In the folder {folder} there are three files you may read:\n    37\t- system.txt: the system message of the request;\n    38\t- user.txt: the user message of the request;\n    39\t- schema.json: the JSON schema your answer must satisfy (strict structured output: every property present, null where a field does not apply).\n    40\tRead those three files and nothing else: no other file in any folder, no web, no tools beyond reading those three files.\n    41\tAct as the model that receives this request: do the analysis the system and user messages ask for, and return your answer as one JSON object that satisfies schema.json exactly.\n    42\tReturn only the JSON object, with no text before or after it.\n    43\t\"\"\"\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\tdef build(out):\n    47\t    if out.exists():\n    48\t        raise SystemExit(f'{out} exists')\n    49\t    for name in CASES:\n    50\t        it = bt.build_case(name, bt.CASES[name])\n    51\t        folder = out / name\n    52\t        (folder / 'answers').mkdir(parents=True)\n    53\t        (folder / 'system.txt').write_text(it['system'])\n    54\t        (folder / 'user.txt').write_text(it['user'])\n    55\t        (folder / 'schema.json').write_text(json.dumps(it['schema'], indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    56\t        (folder / 'item.json').write_text(json.dumps(it, ensure_ascii=False))\n    57\t        (folder / 'writer-prompt.txt').write_text(PROMPT.format(folder=folder))\n    58\t        print(f'{name}: {it[\"arm\"]}, members {it[\"members\"]}, request {len(it[\"system\"]) + len(it[\"user\"])} chars')\n    59\t\n    60\t\n    61\tdef check(out):\n    62\t    rows = []\n    63\t    for name in CASES:\n    64\t        it = json.loads((out / name / 'item.json').read_text())\n    65\t        for w in bt.WRITERS:\n    66\t            path = out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json'\n    67\t            if not path.exists():\n    68\t                raise SystemExit(f'INCOMPLETE: no first answer for {name}/{w}')\n    69\t            raw = path.read_text()\n    70\t            row = {'case': name, 'writer': w, 'sha256': hashlib.sha256(raw.encode()).hexdigest()}\n    71\t            try:\n    72\t                data = bt.first_json(raw)\n    73\t            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n    74\t                data = None\n    75\t            if data is None:\n    76\t                row.update(stage='not JSON', errors=[['format', 'not one JSON object as returned']])\n    77\t            elif model.check_schema(data, it['schema']):\n    78\t                row.update(stage='schema', errors=[['schema', e] for e in model.check_schema(data, it['schema'])])\n    79\t            else:\n    80\t                calc = cs.from_answer(data, it['family'])\n    81\t                result = cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], calc)\n    82\t                row.update(stage='checked', errors=[list(e) for e in result['errors']], recorded=result['recorded'],\n    83\t                           published=result['published'], decision=cs.publish_decision(it['family'], calc, result),\n    84\t                           shapes={a['view_id']: a['shape'] for a in data['answers']})\n    85\t            rows.append(row)\n    86\t    (out / 'CHECKED.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    87\t    for r in rows:\n    88\t        print(f'{r[\"case\"]:5} {r[\"writer\"]:7} {r[\"stage\"]:8} {r.get(\"decision\", \"-\"):9} {r.get(\"shapes\")} '\n    89\t              + '; '.join(f'{c}: {d}' for c, d in r['errors'])[:300])\n    90\t\n    91\t\n    92\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    93\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\tdef _unresolved(answer, why):\n    97\t    a = copy.deepcopy(answer)\n    98\t    for f in a['figures']:\n    99\t        f['outcome_for'], f['reference_for'] = [], []\n   100\t    a['answers'][0].update(shape='unresolved', rung=None, reference_unavailable=None, reasons=why, why=why)\n   101\t    return a\n   102\t\n   103\t\n   104\tdef controls(out):\n   105\t    made = {'K-unsupported': ('E', json.loads((BASE / 'E' / 'answers' / 'codex.json').read_text()))}\n   106\t    made['K-unresolved-unjustified'] = ('P-v8', _unresolved(json.loads((BASE / 'P-v8' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text()),\n   107\t                                                            'The outlook for the second half is uncertain.'))\n   108\t    # Built on the third run's S Codex reply: the S Claude reply is refused by the current code (S1, F11 \"estimated\").\n   109\t    made['K-unresolved-justified'] = ('S', _unresolved(json.loads((BASE / 'S' / 'answers' / 'codex.json').read_text()), (\n   110\t        'The question asks for the four companies\\' data-center capital expenditure, and the rung turns on whether it reaches $700 billion. '\n   111\t        'The passages give the four companies\\' total capital expenditure (about $720-745 billion, e5014; tracked near $745 billion and '\n   112\t        '$760 billion, e1856, e2458), AI-infrastructure spending of more than $730 billion (e1947), and data-center capex of nearly $800 '\n   113\t        'billion for five companies (e5376). None of them gives the four companies\\' data-center share: a total, a broader AI category '\n   114\t        'and a five-company figure do not settle whether the four-company data-center figure is above or below $700 billion, since a '\n   115\t        'share of about 94% of the total is the dividing line. So the rung cannot be settled from these passages.')))\n   116\t    for name, (case, answer) in made.items():\n   117\t        it = json.loads((out / case / 'item.json').read_text())\n   118\t        if model.check_schema(answer, it['schema']):\n   119\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: schema errors {model.check_schema(answer, it[\"schema\"])[:2]}')\n   120\t        codes = sorted({c for c, _ in cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], cs.from_answer(answer, it['family']))['errors']})\n   121\t        if codes:\n   122\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the code refuses it ({codes}); a reader control must be accepted by the code')\n   123\t    return made\n   124\t\n   125\t\n   126\tdef packets(out):\n   127\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n   128\t    items = [{'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'], 'answer': f'{r[\"case\"]}/answers/{r[\"writer\"]}.json'} for r in rows]\n   129\t    folder = out / 'controls'\n   130\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   131\t    for name, (case, answer) in controls(out).items():\n   132\t        (folder / f'{name}.json').write_text(json.dumps(answer, ensure_ascii=False))\n   133\t        items.append({'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': f'controls/{name}.json'})\n   134\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n   135\t    random.Random(hashlib.sha256(b'D745 ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()).shuffle(order)\n   136\t    packets_dir = out / 'reader-packets'\n   137\t    packets_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   138\t    key = {}\n   139\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   140\t        item = items[i]\n   141\t        it = json.loads((out / item['case'] / 'item.json').read_text())\n   142\t        pid = f'U{n:02d}'\n   143\t        key[pid] = item\n   144\t        (packets_dir / f'{pid}.md').write_text('\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(out / item['case']), '',\n   145\t                                                          '# The request as the writer saw it', '', it['user'], '', '# The answer', '',\n   146\t                                                          (out / item['answer']).read_text(), '']))\n   147\t    (out / 'reader-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   148\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {packets_dir}; key in reader-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\tEXPECTED = {'K-unsupported': 'no', 'K-unresolved-unjustified': 'no', 'K-unresolved-justified': 'yes'}\n   152\t\n   153\t\n   154\tdef _reading(path):\n   155\t    text = path.read_text()\n   156\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\tdef _defect_key(defect):\n   160\t    return (defect.get('type'), tuple(sorted(defect.get('figures') or [])))\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\tdef tally(out):\n   164\t    \"\"\"The registered D745 pass lines, scored as written: support settled by the two readers or, where they split, by both\n   165\t    adjudicators; a defect settled when both readers mark it or both adjudicators uphold it.\"\"\"\n   166\t    key = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n   167\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n   168\t    rows, need, settled_defects, split_defects = {}, [], [], []\n   169\t    for pid, item in sorted(key.items()):\n   170\t        first = [_reading(out / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   171\t        adjudicated = [out / 'adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   172\t        adj = [_reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n   173\t        answer = json.loads((out / item['answer']).read_text())\n   174\t        shapes = {a['view_id']: a['shape'] for a in answer['answers']}\n   175\t        marks = [{_defect_key(d) for d in r.get('defects', [])} for r in first]\n   176\t        members = {}\n   177\t        for view in shapes:\n   178\t            votes = [r['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for r in first]\n   179\t            if votes[0] == votes[1]:\n   180\t                members[view] = (votes[0], votes)\n   181\t            elif adj is None:\n   182\t                members[view] = ('needs adjudication', votes)\n   183\t            else:\n   184\t                second = [a['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for a in adj]\n   185\t                members[view] = (second[0] if second[0] == second[1] else 'split', votes + second)\n   186\t        both = marks[0] & marks[1]\n   187\t        either = (marks[0] | marks[1]) - both\n   188\t        for d in both:\n   189\t            settled_defects.append([pid, item['case'], item['writer'], d[0], list(d[1]), 'both readers'])\n   190\t        if either and adj is None:\n   191\t            need.append(pid)\n   192\t        elif either:\n   193\t            decisions = [{_defect_key(d): d.get('decision') for d in a.get('defects', [])} for a in adj]\n   194\t            for d in sorted(either, key=str):\n   195\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   196\t                row = [pid, item['case'], item['writer'], d[0], list(d[1]), pair]\n   197\t                if pair == ['uphold', 'uphold']:\n   198\t                    settled_defects.append(row)\n   199\t                elif pair != ['reject', 'reject']:       # a split, or a defect an adjudicator did not decide: read by hand\n   200\t                    split_defects.append(row)\n   201\t        if any(v[0] == 'needs adjudication' for v in members.values()) and pid not in need:\n   202\t            need.append(pid)\n   203\t        rows[pid] = {'case': item['case'], 'writer': item['writer'], 'shapes': shapes, 'members': members}\n   204\t    if need:\n   205\t        print('adjudication needed for:', ' '.join(sorted(need)))\n   206\t        return\n   207\t    lines, verdict = [], 'pass'\n   208\t    for pid, r in rows.items():\n   209\t        for view, (value, votes) in r['members'].items():\n   210\t            shape = r['shapes'][view]\n   211\t            if r['writer'] in EXPECTED:\n   212\t                ok = value == EXPECTED[r['writer']]\n   213\t                rule = f'control expects {EXPECTED[r[\"writer\"]]}'\n   214\t            elif r['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   215\t                ok = shape == 'forecast' and value == 'yes'\n   216\t                rule = 'a supported forecast'\n   217\t            else:\n   218\t                ok = value == 'yes'\n   219\t                rule = 'a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer'\n   220\t            if value == 'split':\n   221\t                verdict = 'inconclusive' if verdict == 'pass' else verdict\n   222\t            elif not ok:\n   223\t                verdict = 'fail'\n   224\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {r[\"case\"]:5} {r[\"writer\"]:25} {view} {shape:10} settled={value:6} votes={votes} [{rule}] '\n   225\t                         f'{\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   226\t    stops = [d for d in settled_defects if d[2] not in EXPECTED]\n   227\t    refused = [k for k, v in checked.items() if v.get('decision') != 'publish']\n   228\t    if stops or refused:\n   229\t        verdict = 'fail'\n   230\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'lines': lines, 'settled_defects': settled_defects, 'split_defects': split_defects,\n   231\t              'refused': refused}\n   232\t    (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   233\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   234\t    print('settled defects:', settled_defects)\n   235\t    print('split defects (disclosed):', split_defects)\n   236\t    print('refused first replies:', refused)\n   237\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   241\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) == 3 else ''\n   242\t    target = Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve() if len(sys.argv) == 3 else None\n   243\t    {'build': build, 'check': check, 'packets': packets, 'tally': tally}.get(\n   244\t        cmd, lambda _: (_ for _ in ()).throw(SystemExit(__doc__)))(target)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | sed -n '194,254p;848,905p'; rg -n 'cap|cost|\\\\\"'$[0-9]|36'\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md | head -24; rg -n 'def (check|publish_decision)|S[1-8]|assumption' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | tail -55\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json | sed -n '194,254p;848,905p'; rg -n 'cap|cost|\\\\\"'$[0-9]|36'\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md | head -24; rg -n 'def (check|publish_decision)|S[1-8]|assumption' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | tail -55\"","aggregated_output":"   194\t    ]\n   195\t   },\n   196\t   \"reasoning\": \"The company guides Q2 non-GAAP gross margin to about 75%, described as holding at 75% (i281, i130, i153), after 74.9% in Q1 (i163, i084), so half the year is fixed near 75. The second half is the Rubin ramp (i219): Morgan Stanley puts memory at 25-30% of the VR200 rack bill of materials against 5-10% on GB200, about $2m of memory per rack, up 435% (i217), bought in a market Micron says stays tight until 2028 at 86% supplier margins (i252, i142). Memory passed through at a lower markup dilutes the percentage even if NVIDIA holds its $55,000 GPU price (i218), while hyperscalers self-sourcing memory (i220) would trim revenue and restore the mix. I bet 74-76% for the year, in line with the 75% guide. The strongest risk is that the memory cost is not fully passed through (i217, i142).\"\n   197\t  },\n   198\t  {\n   199\t   \"question_id\": \"q04\",\n   200\t   \"question\": \"How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\",\n   201\t   \"kind\": \"theme\",\n   202\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   203\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v4\",\n   204\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n   205\t   \"no_view\": false,\n   206\t   \"reference\": {\n   207\t    \"text\": \"The big four's own plans: around $650bn this year (i256, i089, i253), the figure NVIDIA management also cites (i173).\",\n   208\t    \"value\": 650000000000,\n   209\t    \"items\": [\n   210\t     \"i256\",\n   211\t     \"i089\",\n   212\t     \"i173\",\n   213\t     \"i253\"\n   214\t    ]\n   215\t   },\n   216\t   \"implied_outcome\": {\n   217\t    \"text\": \"Alphabet alone guides up to $190bn (i232); Google Cloud backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn (i233) and Microsoft's remaining performance obligations are $627bn (i039), so plans drift up rather than down; memory prices sharply higher and tight until 2028 (i252, i143) lift the dollar cost of the same build; NVIDIA reports accelerating growth (i295) and SemiAnalysis sees second-half shipments above market estimates (i111). Against that, more than 75 US projects worth about $130bn have been blocked or delayed this year (i146, i041). I bet $650-720bn.\",\n   218\t    \"low\": 650000000000,\n   219\t    \"high\": 720000000000,\n   220\t    \"yes_no\": null,\n   221\t    \"items\": [\n   222\t     \"i232\",\n   223\t     \"i233\",\n   224\t     \"i039\",\n   225\t     \"i252\",\n   226\t     \"i295\",\n   227\t     \"i111\",\n   228\t     \"i146\"\n   229\t    ]\n   230\t   },\n   231\t   \"direction\": \"in_line\",\n   232\t   \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n   233\t   \"decisive_items\": [\n   234\t    \"i256\",\n   235\t    \"i232\",\n   236\t    \"i233\",\n   237\t    \"i295\"\n   238\t   ],\n   239\t   \"against\": {\n   240\t    \"text\": \"Local opposition has blocked or delayed more than 75 US data-center projects worth about $130bn so far this year (i146, i041), and Meta's CEO has spoken of slower-than-expected progress in AI agents (i292).\",\n   241\t    \"items\": [\n   242\t     \"i146\",\n   243\t     \"i292\"\n   244\t    ]\n   245\t   },\n   246\t   \"reasoning\": \"The big four's own plans are around $650bn for 2026 (i256, i089, i253), and NVIDIA management uses the same figure (i173). Alphabet guides up to $190bn (i232), Google Cloud's backlog nearly doubled to over $460bn (i233) and Microsoft's remaining performance obligations are $627bn (i039), so the contracted demand behind the plans is growing, and memory prices sharply higher and tight until 2028 (i252, i143) raise the dollar cost of the same build. NVIDIA reports accelerating growth as it nears $100bn quarters (i295) and SemiAnalysis sees second-half shipments above market estimates (i111), which is what the hyperscalers' cheques pay for. Against that, more than 75 US data-center projects worth about $130bn have been blocked or delayed this year (i146, i041), and Meta's CEO has spoken of slower-than-expected AI-agent progress (i292). I bet $650-720bn: in line with the plan, with the drift upward.\"\n   247\t  },\n   248\t  {\n   249\t   \"question_id\": \"q05\",\n   250\t   \"question\": \"How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?\",\n   251\t   \"kind\": \"theme\",\n   252\t   \"unit\": \"percent change 2027 vs 2026 (10 means up 10%)\",\n   253\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v5\",\n   254\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n   848\t   },\n   849\t   \"reasoning\": \"NVIDIA's roadmap has Kyber and Rubin Ultra in 2027 (i277, i101). SemiAnalysis reports, with a mechanism, that the Kyber NVL144 rack slipped more than twelve months to 2028 because the PCB midplane cannot yet be manufactured (i100, i177, i189, i196; one claim carried by many outlets), that the NVL72x2 stopgap was scrapped after customer pushback (i102, i178), that the four-die Rubin Ultra was dropped for a two-die part (i179, i192, i200), that CPO-NVSwitch slips to the Feynman generation (i180, i201), and that NVIDIA will cover the gap by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks (i181). NVIDIA's reply is three words, 'Our roadmap is intact' (i101, i193, i211), and Tom's Hardware notes it is unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant (i197); suppliers' shares fell on the report (i177, i194). A 2027 product with a specific delay report needs only a small slip to miss December 31, 2027, and the question also requires the Kyber NVL144 form. I bet no, with low confidence: the material does not settle it, and the company's denial (i101, i247) is the strongest item against.\"\n   850\t  },\n   851\t  {\n   852\t   \"question_id\": \"q19\",\n   853\t   \"question\": \"How much revenue will NVIDIA report from standalone Vera CPUs in FY2027?\",\n   854\t   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   855\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   856\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v19\",\n   857\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n   858\t   \"no_view\": false,\n   859\t   \"reference\": {\n   860\t    \"text\": \"Management's own figure: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080): one claim retold three times.\",\n   861\t    \"value\": 20000000000,\n   862\t    \"items\": [\n   863\t     \"i288\",\n   864\t     \"i095\",\n   865\t     \"i080\"\n   866\t    ]\n   867\t   },\n   868\t   \"implied_outcome\": {\n   869\t    \"text\": \"The first stand-alone CPU launches this fall (i096), so the revenue falls in roughly two quarters; at Morgan Stanley's $5,000 per Vera CPU (i218), $20bn is four million units. Demand signals are real: Perplexity plans to deploy Vera in production (i262, i291) and Intel's CEO sees the CPU:GPU ratio in inference moving toward 1:1 (i009); but NVIDIA chose Intel Xeon 6 as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8 (i008). I bet $8-18bn, below the $20bn ambition.\",\n   870\t    \"low\": 8000000000,\n   871\t    \"high\": 18000000000,\n   872\t    \"yes_no\": null,\n   873\t    \"items\": [\n   874\t     \"i096\",\n   875\t     \"i218\",\n   876\t     \"i262\",\n   877\t     \"i291\",\n   878\t     \"i009\",\n   879\t     \"i008\"\n   880\t    ]\n   881\t   },\n   882\t   \"direction\": \"below\",\n   883\t   \"confidence\": \"low\",\n   884\t   \"decisive_items\": [\n   885\t    \"i288\",\n   886\t    \"i095\",\n   887\t    \"i096\",\n   888\t    \"i218\"\n   889\t   ],\n   890\t   \"against\": {\n   891\t    \"text\": \"Management expects $20bn by fiscal year-end (i288, i095), and Intel's CEO describes inference moving toward one CPU per GPU (i009).\",\n   892\t    \"items\": [\n   893\t     \"i288\",\n   894\t     \"i009\"\n   895\t    ]\n   896\t   },\n   897\t   \"reasoning\": \"The reference is management's own number: $20bn of stand-alone CPU sales this year (i095), by the end of this fiscal year (i288), nearly $20bn with no period (i080), one claim retold three times. The first stand-alone CPU launches this fall (i096), so the revenue falls in roughly two quarters, and at Morgan Stanley's $5,000 per Vera CPU (i218) $20bn is four million units. Demand signals are real: Perplexity plans to deploy Vera in production after a 1.5x result on agentic coding (i262, i291), and Intel's CEO sees the CPU:GPU ratio in inference moving from 1:8 toward 1:1 (i009); but NVIDIA itself chose Intel Xeon 6 as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8 (i008). Lean cautious on a product with no shipments yet: I bet $8-18bn, below the $20bn ambition, with the top of the range reachable if the fall ramp is steep. Management's $20bn (i288, i095) is the strongest item against.\"\n   898\t  },\n   899\t  {\n   900\t   \"question_id\": \"q20\",\n   901\t   \"question\": \"How much revenue will NVIDIA report from Blackwell and Rubin products in FY2027?\",\n   902\t   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   903\t   \"unit\": \"USD\",\n   904\t   \"seed_view_id\": \"v20\",\n   905\t   \"source\": \"reader B\",\n3:**Result (D733): STOP.** All 72 answers were refused, and $0.3677 was spent; see `PAID-GATE-RESULT.md`.\n15:| single-S (old-2 v6, four hyperscalers' 2027 capex) | single | v6 | 31 |\n21:- **Draws:** three per request, so 36 first attempts.\n32:- **Estimate** (at `hivemind.model.PRICES`: gpt-6-luna, $0.10 input and $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as\n35:  - the 36 first attempts cost about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n36:  - the transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request.\n37:  - **Proposed ceiling: $0.50.** It covers every scored attempt. Retries come last (§3g), so a ceiling stop among them leaves the\n42:## 3. The $0 rehearsal (`paid_rehearsal.py`, `rehearsal.log`: 16 of 16)\n44:- **Clean run:** 36 first attempts, every HTTP body byte-equal to an independently built request, every unit published, the separate\n78:- **Rehearsal:** 27 of 27 (`rehearsal.log`). The $0 gate still passes (`gate-run27.txt`: 87 of 87), and the mutants are unchanged.\n219:  - The run sends every first attempt (all 36) before any retry.\n222:  - Rehearsed: an interruption during a retry leaves all 36 scored attempts and a complete verdict. A transport failure on a retry\n249:  - One mutant (splitting on any line break) is caught when a scenario guard stops the rehearsal: 36 attempts started, 35 finished.\n282:- **STOP or INCOMPLETE:** the line goes back to Alex with the readings, and nothing is tuned on these 36 answers.\n375:        errors.append(('S7', f'{fid} is defined twice'))\n379:            errors.append(('S7', 'a figure has no id'))\n382:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure id must not be a passage id'))\n384:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: kind, basis or unit outside the closed set'))\n388:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a number that is not finite or beyond {LIMIT:g}'))\n393:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure has value, or low and high, never both and never one end alone'))\n395:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: low is above high'))\n398:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\n400:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs and operation are for computed figures only'))\n402:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on is for estimates only'))\n405:                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source cannot have basis forecast'))\n407:                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source needs evidence_id and quote_number'))\n411:                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a computed figure needs inputs and an operation from the closed set'))\n415:                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: {op} takes ' + (f'exactly {lo_n}' if hi_n == lo_n else f'{lo_n} or more') + f' inputs, not {len(ins)}'))\n418:                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs name passage {i}; inputs name figures only'))\n420:                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: input {i} is not a figure'))\n422:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: an estimate needs basis_text'))\n425:                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on names {r}, which is neither a figure nor a shown passage'))\n439:        errors.append(('S7', 'a figure depends on itself (through inputs or rests_on)'))\n446:            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} has {seen.get(v, 0)} answers; exactly one is required'))\n449:            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} is not a member of the family'))\n460:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: evidence_id {ev} is not a shown passage'))\n467:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is part of the longer expression \"{inside[0]}\" in {ev}; copy it whole' if inside else\n472:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not a US-dollar amount, a percentage or double, triple or twice, as written'))\n475:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is in {parsed[\"unit\"]}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n485:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" gives {want} {parsed[\"unit\"]}'))\n496:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\n509:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} cannot take inputs in {\", \".join(figs[i][\"unit\"] for i in ins)}{hint}'))\n512:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} of these inputs gives {unit}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n520:                errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: divides by a figure whose value can be zero'))\n523:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: a share takes a percentage from 0 to 100'))\n527:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n537:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: declared {declared} but {op} of {\", \".join(ins)} gives {got_text}'))\n562:            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: its outcome {out[\"id\"]} must be your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast)'))\n564:            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} measures {\" | \".join(_key(out[\"what\"]))}, not the question\\'s {\" | \".join(_key(member[\"what\"]))}'))\n566:            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} is in {out[\"unit\"]}, not {member.get(\"unit\", \"USD bn\")}'))\n570:            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung!r} is not one of 1..{len(member[\"bands\"])}'))\n575:            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung} {member[\"bands\"][rung - 1]} does not hold its outcome {shown} as recomputed'))\n580:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on an estimate'))\n582:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on a forecast'))\n584:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} has no quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'))\n589:                    errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} carries the reported {\", \".join(carried)} of another period to '\n627:            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\n647:                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n651:                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\", \".join(bypass)} for {\" | \".join(key)} beside its forecast figure {node[0]}'))\n676:                errors.append(('S3', f'{label}: the part {part} ({pv[0]:g}–{pv[1]:g}) exceeds the whole {whole} ({wv[0]:g}–{wv[1]:g})'))\n695:                    errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: adds {ps[\"part\"]} into {ps[\"whole\"]} for the same period, which already includes it'))\n699:                errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: a difference may take {ps[\"part\"]} from {ps[\"whole\"]} of the same period only to give the rest'))\n706:            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: adds one quantity for the same period more than once ({\", \".join(inputs_periods)})'))\n714:            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: when periods of one quantity are added or subtracted, write each as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or '\n728:            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: {sign.join(periods[:-1])} does not make up {periods[-1]}'))\n738:            recorded.append({'type': 'estimate', 'figure': fid, 'assumption': f.get('basis_text')})\n764:def check(family, passages, calc):\n773:            errors.append(('S2', f'{f[\"id\"]}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n782:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
