{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && nl -ba reader-key.json && nl -ba TALLY.json && nl -ba CHECKED.json | head -160 && for f in {S,E,P-v8}/schema.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'estimate|reasons|overlaps|unresolved|bound' \"'$f; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\n     2\t \"U01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-unjustified\",\n     5\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json\"\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"U02\": {\n     8\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     9\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    10\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/claude.json\"\n    11\t },\n    12\t \"U03\": {\n    13\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    14\t  \"writer\": \"K-unsupported\",\n    15\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unsupported.json\"\n    16\t },\n    17\t \"U04\": {\n    18\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-justified\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-justified.json\"\n    21\t },\n    22\t \"U05\": {\n    23\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    24\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    25\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/codex.json\"\n    26\t },\n    27\t \"U06\": {\n    28\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    29\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    30\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/claude.json\"\n    31\t },\n    32\t \"U07\": {\n    33\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    34\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    35\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/codex.json\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"U08\": {\n    38\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    39\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    40\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/codex.json\"\n    41\t },\n    42\t \"U09\": {\n    43\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    44\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    45\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/claude.json\"\n    46\t }\n    47\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n     3\t \"lines\": [\n     4\t  \"U01 P-v8  K-unresolved-unjustified  v8 unresolved settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects no] ok\",\n     5\t  \"U02 S     claude                    v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n     6\t  \"U03 E     K-unsupported             v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects no] ok\",\n     7\t  \"U04 S     K-unresolved-justified    v6 unresolved settled=split  votes=['yes', 'no', 'yes', 'no'] [control expects yes] MISS\",\n     8\t  \"U05 E     codex                     v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n     9\t  \"U06 E     claude                    v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    10\t  \"U07 P-v8  codex                     v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    11\t  \"U08 S     codex                     v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    12\t  \"U09 P-v8  claude                    v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\"\n    13\t ],\n    14\t \"settled_defects\": [],\n    15\t \"split_defects\": [],\n    16\t \"refused\": []\n    17\t}     1\t[\n     2\t {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n     5\t  \"sha256\": \"d81bc24f57330d34e985b9e4069fd84d8e231b8f981bb02ac291de8b61bfc221\",\n     6\t  \"stage\": \"checked\",\n     7\t  \"errors\": [],\n     8\t  \"recorded\": [\n     9\t   {\n    10\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n    11\t    \"figure\": \"F4\"\n    12\t   },\n    13\t   {\n    14\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    15\t    \"figure\": \"F8\",\n    16\t    \"qualifier\": \"roughly\"\n    17\t   },\n    18\t   {\n    19\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n    20\t    \"figure\": \"F9\"\n    21\t   },\n    22\t   {\n    23\t    \"type\": \"estimate\",\n    24\t    \"figure\": \"F10\",\n    25\t    \"assumption\": \"My estimate of Microsoft's calendar-2026 capital expenditure; no calendar-year guidance is shown. The January-March 2026 quarter (fiscal Q3) was $30.88bn, up 84% from a year earlier (F5, F6). A continued sequential rise of about 16% a quarter, the pace that compounds to roughly 84% over a year and is supported by the $678bn Azure backlog (F7), gives about 30.9 + 35.8 + 41.6 + 48.2 = $156bn; the four-company tally less the three guided companies implies $150-205bn (F9). $165bn follows the run-rate path and moves partway into the tally-implied range, staying in its lower part because the run-rate is the only Microsoft-specific number.\"\n    26\t   },\n    27\t   {\n    28\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n    29\t    \"figure\": \"F11\"\n    30\t   },\n    31\t   {\n    32\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    33\t    \"figure\": \"F12\",\n    34\t    \"qualifier\": \"more than\"\n    35\t   },\n    36\t   {\n    37\t    \"type\": \"estimate\",\n    38\t    \"figure\": \"F14\",\n    39\t    \"assumption\": \"My estimate of the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending. The companies' indicated AI-infrastructure total, more than $730bn (F12), is at or above the total-capex tally of $720-745bn (F8), which puts the share near 100%; Meta's planned AI capex of $130bn (F13) sits inside its $125-145bn total guidance (F2), about 96% of the midpoint; Amazon's raise to $220bn was mostly memory-chip costs, i.e. server hardware (e1649); outside estimates put spending on data centers at about $750bn (e1416, e4082). No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual; the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%.\"\n    40\t   },\n    41\t   {\n    42\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    43\t    \"figure\": \"F16\",\n    44\t    \"qualifier\": \"near\"\n    45\t   },\n    46\t   {\n    47\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    48\t    \"figure\": \"F18\",\n    49\t    \"qualifier\": \"estimated\"\n    50\t   },\n    51\t   {\n    52\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    53\t    \"figure\": \"F19\",\n    54\t    \"qualifier\": \"nearly\"\n    55\t   },\n    56\t   {\n    57\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    58\t    \"figure\": \"F20\",\n    59\t    \"qualifier\": \"roughly\"\n    60\t   }\n    61\t  ],\n    62\t  \"published\": {\n    63\t   \"v6\": [\n    64\t    690.9,\n    65\t    720.3,\n    66\t    null\n    67\t   ]\n    68\t  },\n    69\t  \"decision\": \"publish\",\n    70\t  \"shapes\": {\n    71\t   \"v6\": \"forecast\"\n    72\t  }\n    73\t },\n    74\t {\n    75\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    76\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    77\t  \"sha256\": \"b463cec04f0ff75ad290de28c0c0d2d1443c79660bea552c08fa5f6d29bc86b8\",\n    78\t  \"stage\": \"checked\",\n    79\t  \"errors\": [],\n    80\t  \"recorded\": [\n    81\t   {\n    82\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    83\t    \"figure\": \"F1\",\n    84\t    \"qualifier\": \"more than\"\n    85\t   },\n    86\t   {\n    87\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    88\t    \"figure\": \"F2\",\n    89\t    \"qualifier\": \"roughly\"\n    90\t   },\n    91\t   {\n    92\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    93\t    \"figure\": \"F3\",\n    94\t    \"qualifier\": \"near\"\n    95\t   },\n    96\t   {\n    97\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    98\t    \"figure\": \"F10\",\n    99\t    \"qualifier\": \"nearly\"\n   100\t   },\n   101\t   {\n   102\t    \"type\": \"estimate\",\n   103\t    \"figure\": \"F11\",\n   104\t    \"assumption\": \"I forecast spending at the upper end of the Zacks range. Alphabet, Amazon and Meta lifted their full-year plans, and Microsoft's reported quarter and strong cloud demand support a rising spending pace. Amazon's higher memory costs lift dollar spending even if equipment volumes rise more slowly. Project delays and grid constraints make the higher plan tally less likely to be fully spent this year. The annual estimates already reflect the company plans, so I use those plans to choose where spending lands within the estimates.\"\n   105\t   },\n   106\t   {\n   107\t    \"type\": \"estimate\",\n   108\t    \"figure\": \"F12\",\n   109\t    \"assumption\": \"I estimate a $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers. The four-company AI infrastructure tally is close to my total-capex forecast, and Meta's AI capex estimate is close to the midpoint of its companywide guidance. Those comparisons support a small deduction. I allow room for different spending mixes across the companies and for some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The passages do not give a company-by-company split.\"\n   110\t   },\n   111\t   {\n   112\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n   113\t    \"figure\": \"F13\"\n   114\t   },\n   115\t   {\n   116\t    \"type\": \"one quantity, several figures\",\n   117\t    \"figure\": \"F11\",\n   118\t    \"figures\": [\n   119\t     \"F11\",\n   120\t     \"F2\",\n   121\t     \"F3\",\n   122\t     \"F4\"\n   123\t    ],\n   124\t    \"what\": [\n   125\t     \"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   126\t     \"capital expenditures\",\n   127\t     \"combined total\",\n   128\t     \"CY2026\"\n   129\t    ]\n   130\t   }\n   131\t  ],\n   132\t  \"published\": {\n   133\t   \"v6\": [\n   134\t    720.0,\n   135\t    720.0,\n   136\t    null\n   137\t   ]\n   138\t  },\n   139\t  \"decision\": \"publish\",\n   140\t  \"shapes\": {\n   141\t   \"v6\": \"forecast\"\n   142\t  }\n   143\t },\n   144\t {\n   145\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n   146\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n   147\t  \"sha256\": \"9c4f314cba4a64560845fb26addeffa95cb22e8dc3861d8a5d95cea8d8cbc962\",\n   148\t  \"stage\": \"checked\",\n   149\t  \"errors\": [],\n   150\t  \"recorded\": [\n   151\t   {\n   152\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n   153\t    \"figure\": \"F2\",\n   154\t    \"qualifier\": \"more than\"\n   155\t   },\n   156\t   {\n   157\t    \"type\": \"carried across periods\",\n   158\t    \"figure\": \"F3\"\n   159\t   },\n   160\t   {\nS/schema.json\n45:       \"estimate\"\n47:      \"description\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\"\n58:      \"description\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"\n186:         \"estimated\",\n275:      \"description\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"\n285:      \"description\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"\n309:   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\"\n326:       \"unresolved\"\n328:      \"description\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\"\n334:        \"description\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\"\n352:     \"reasons\": {\n533:     \"reasons\",\nE/schema.json\n45:       \"estimate\"\n47:      \"description\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\"\n58:      \"description\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"\n151:         \"estimated\",\n240:      \"description\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"\n250:      \"description\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"\n274:   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\"\n291:       \"unresolved\"\n293:      \"description\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\"\n299:        \"description\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\"\n317:     \"reasons\": {\n437:     \"reasons\",\nP-v8/schema.json\n45:       \"estimate\"\n47:      \"description\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\"\n58:      \"description\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"\n432:         \"estimated\",\n521:      \"description\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"\n531:      \"description\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"\n555:   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\"\n572:       \"unresolved\"\n574:      \"description\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\"\n580:        \"description\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\"\n598:     \"reasons\": {\n1277:     \"reasons\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in {E,S}/answers/{codex,claude}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'\\\"basis_text\\\": \\\"|\\\"why\\\": \\\"' \"'$f; done; rg -n '\"'scope|data.center|broader|AI infrastructure' S/user.txt | tail -22\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in {E,S}/answers/{codex,claude}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'\\\"basis_text\\\": \\\"|\\\"why\\\": \\\"' \"'$f; done; rg -n '\"'scope|data.center|broader|AI infrastructure' S/user.txt | tail -22\"","aggregated_output":"E/answers/codex.json\n94:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the reference. The announced breadth of deployments supports most of the ramp. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, and AMD's agentic-workload tests show a competitive alternative. I allow for some planned sales to slip or go to a rival, equivalent to less than half an average quarter of revenue at the target pace. The passages give no order sizes or shipment pace to narrow that estimate.\",\nE/answers/claude.json\n93:      \"basis_text\": \"My forecast of FY2028 CPU revenue growth over FY2027 is 1.8x. Management's outlook of more than 2x (F2) is a full-year target for the next fiscal year: all four selling quarters are still ahead, and it rests on a Vera ramp that no passage yet shows in volume. So it is discounted for time and execution. Anthropic is only evaluating Vera (e5044), deployments are described in the future tense (e4689), and AMD claims its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404, a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal). The lower anchor is the outside market pace of about 50% a year (F4; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 in e4857 is a similar pace), which is what a vendor that only holds its share would reach (1.5x). NVIDIA's claim of deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above that pace. 1.8x sits between the market pace (1.5x) and the guidance floor (2.0x), weighted toward the guidance because the CFO reaffirmed it.\",\n131:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate Vera at 90% of NVIDIA's FY2028 CPU revenue. Management attributes the FY2028 doubling to Vera itself (e4691: 'Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year'; e4856), and Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689), so Vera is most of the year's CPU revenue. The remainder is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. No passage sizes that remainder. The rung holds for any share from about 84% up to 100% at the forecast pace.\",\nS/answers/codex.json\n207:      \"basis_text\": \"I forecast spending at the upper end of the Zacks range. Alphabet, Amazon and Meta lifted their full-year plans, and Microsoft's reported quarter and strong cloud demand support a rising spending pace. Amazon's higher memory costs lift dollar spending even if equipment volumes rise more slowly. Project delays and grid constraints make the higher plan tally less likely to be fully spent this year. The annual estimates already reflect the company plans, so I use those plans to choose where spending lands within the estimates.\",\n226:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate a $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers. The four-company AI infrastructure tally is close to my total-capex forecast, and Meta's AI capex estimate is close to the midpoint of its companywide guidance. Those comparisons support a small deduction. I allow room for different spending mixes across the companies and for some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The passages do not give a company-by-company split.\",\nS/answers/claude.json\n188:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of Microsoft's calendar-2026 capital expenditure; no calendar-year guidance is shown. The January-March 2026 quarter (fiscal Q3) was $30.88bn, up 84% from a year earlier (F5, F6). A continued sequential rise of about 16% a quarter, the pace that compounds to roughly 84% over a year and is supported by the $678bn Azure backlog (F7), gives about 30.9 + 35.8 + 41.6 + 48.2 = $156bn; the four-company tally less the three guided companies implies $150-205bn (F9). $165bn follows the run-rate path and moves partway into the tally-implied range, staying in its lower part because the run-rate is the only Microsoft-specific number.\",\n264:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending. The companies' indicated AI-infrastructure total, more than $730bn (F12), is at or above the total-capex tally of $720-745bn (F8), which puts the share near 100%; Meta's planned AI capex of $130bn (F13) sits inside its $125-145bn total guidance (F2), about 96% of the midpoint; Amazon's raise to $220bn was mostly memory-chip costs, i.e. server hardware (e1649); outside estimates put spending on data centers at about $750bn (e1416, e4082). No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual; the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%.\",\n17:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n19:- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n20:    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n30:    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n47:- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n48:    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n57:- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n58:    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n75:- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n76:    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n83:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n84:    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n111:    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n117:- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n118:    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n119:- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n120:    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n121:- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n123:- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n128:- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n129:    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in reread2-packets/T{02,06,10,12,15,17}.{claude,codex}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'ground|why_not' \"'$f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in reread2-packets/T{02,06,10,12,15,17}.{claude,codex}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'ground|why_not' \"'$f; done'","aggregated_output":"reread2-packets/T02.claude.json\n1:{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e2620 (NVIDIA reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026, F1) less e2889 (NVIDIA's same-day statement that $18 billion is committed for the rest of the fiscal year, F2, read as the part of the July book funded by year end) gives about $7 billion still owed from the July book (F3); plus the stated estimate F4 of $2-13 billion of post-July commitments still unfunded at year end, built from the staged or unclosed amounts in e1970 (SB Energy second $1.5 billion at IPO), e1919 (Lancium milestone $1 billion), e4648 (Anthropic IPO anchor up to $10 billion) and e3934 (Thinking Machines about $2.5 billion), on the stated assumption that Poolside (e2207), MediaTek (e3474) and SB Energy's first tranche (e1957) fund at closing, with e2643 and e4487 arguing against further large staged commitments. F5 = $9-20 billion, wholly below the $25 billion rung 1 boundary.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread2-packets/T02.codex.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"The $9–20 billion forecast assumes that e2889's $18 billion will be funded by fiscal year-end entirely from e2620's $25 billion July 26 balance, leaving $7 billion. It adds an estimated $2–13 billion in new commitments still unfunded at year-end, based mainly on e1970, e1919, and e4648.\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"e2889 says $18 billion was committed for the rest of the fiscal year. It does not establish that the full amount was included in the July 26 balance or will be paid by year-end. The deal passages describe possible or conditional investments without establishing when they would be signed or funded. They do not support the $13 billion upper estimate for new unpaid commitments. e2643 concerns whether large AI-lab investments will repeat next year and does not bound commitments made before this fiscal year ends.\"\nreread2-packets/T06.claude.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"F12, the writer's own 90% data-center share of total capital expenditure (resting on F1/e5014 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure', F5/e1549 Meta capex guidance $125-145bn, F6/e3686 Meta AI capex $130bn, and e4404), applied by share() to F11, a $745bn total-capex estimate taken from the top of the Zacks range (F1/e5014, also e2079) and below the $760bn plan (F2/e2458). 745 x 90% = 670.5, which is in rung 3.\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"The rung depends entirely on the 90% share. On a $745bn total, data-center capex reaches $700bn at a share of about 94%, so the forecast only stays in rung 3 because of the gap between 90% and 94%. The passages do not support a share that low. The writer's own Meta comparison gives 130/135, about 96%, at the guidance midpoint (89.7% to 104% across the range), which is above the cutoff. NVIDIA gives the same 'nearly $800 billion' as the top five's total capex (e2759, e5081) and as their data-center capex (e5376, e4766), so in that source the data-center spend is close to the total. e1416 and e4082 put data-center spending at about $750bn, which is in rung 4. No passage gives any non-data-center spending to subtract. The 10% deduction is the writer's unsourced 'allowance for other capital spending', and e4404 (AWS growth) says nothing about the spending mix. The $745bn total is well grounded, but the assumption that puts the forecast below $700bn is not.\"\nreread2-packets/T06.codex.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"The answer applies an assumed 90% data-center share to $745 billion of total capex, the high end of e5014, to forecast $670.5 billion in rung 3.\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"e5014 says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure but gives no data-center share. The comparison of Meta's AI capex estimate in e3686 with its total capex guidance in e1549 gives no data-center split for Meta or the four companies combined. e4404 reports AWS growth without a spending breakdown. At $745 billion of total capex, a data-center share of about 94% would cross into rung 4, and the passages do not establish which side of that cutoff is more likely.\"\nreread2-packets/T10.claude.json\n1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Build of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure from the shown guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2), Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3), plus a stated estimate for Microsoft of $175bn (F6), set between a run-rate build from its January-March 2026 quarter of $30.88bn up 84% (e922; 15% sequential growth gives about $154bn, checked) and the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 $745bn, e2458 $760bn) less the other three companies' guidance ($540-570bn); combined $715-745bn (F7, sum checked). The stated 95-100% data-center share (F13) rests on outside sources labelling the same totals as data-center spending (NVIDIA's top-five nearly $800bn is called capex in e2759 and data-center capex in e4766/e5376; e4082 around $750bn of AI data-center spending); share gives $679.25-745bn (F14, checked), midpoint about $712bn, rung 4 chosen as a band the range overlaps with the reason given. Reference e5014 ($720-745bn, four companies) also sits in rung 4. A reader can check every step; the rung-4 call leans on two stated assumptions (Microsoft at $175bn rather than the ~$155bn run-rate, and a near-total data-center share, against e5014's weaker 'large portion'), and the basis text's '$175-190bn at their centres' understates the top of the residual range (the $760bn tally gives about $205bn), which does not weaken the chosen value.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread2-packets/T10.codex.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"F6 estimates $175 billion in Microsoft capital expenditure using e922, e4405 and outside estimates of the four companies’ total spending. F13 assumes 95–100% of combined capital expenditure goes to data centers, citing e1649, e5014, e1416, e4082 and e5376. That yields $679.25–745 billion, with a midpoint of $712.125 billion in rung 4.\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"The passages support substantial data-center spending, but they do not establish that other spending accounts for at most 5% of the four companies’ combined capital expenditure. e1649 explains Amazon’s increase, and e5014 says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure without sizing it. The data-center estimates cover a broader or unspecified group. F8 is an outside estimate of total capital expenditure, so it does not independently show what the market expects for the data-center portion.\"\nreread2-packets/T12.claude.json\n1:{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e2620 (reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026, FY2027 Q2 end) and e2889 ($18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of the fiscal year), read as the scheduled H2 funding of the July balance; stated assumption F8 that $17 billion of it funds before year-end, leaving F9 = 25 - 17 = $8 billion; plus stated estimate F10 that $8 billion of post-July commitments (SB Energy e1957/e2404, Lancium e1919, Poolside e2207, the Anthropic and Thinking Machines talks e4648/e3934, discounted because most fund at closing or have uncertain signing dates) is still unfunded at year-end; F11 = $16 billion, below the $25 billion edge of rung 1. The weakest link is F10, a judgement not built from the sized deals (their full sizes, roughly $18-20 billion, would cross $25 billion only if all were signed and none funded by year-end), but it is stated as an estimate with its basis and a reader can check it against the passages.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread2-packets/T12.codex.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"The $16 billion forecast starts with the $25 billion July balance in e2620, subtracts $17 billion in assumed funding based on e2889 and e4729, and adds $8 billion in assumed new commitments still unfunded at year-end based on the announced and potential deals.\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"e2889 gives $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year but does not establish how much belongs to the July balance or when it will be funded. e4729 reports past purchases. The deal passages support continued investment activity but do not show which deals will be signed and still unfunded at year-end. The evidence therefore does not establish that funding from the July balance will exceed new commitments still outstanding, the condition needed to finish below $25 billion.\"\nreread2-packets/T15.claude.json\n1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F8 = apply_growth(F1 $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue from e3660, F7 85%) = 37, rung 3 ($30-40bn). The band rests on F7's stated assumption: management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856; repeated in e4691, e4764) is discounted for time and execution because it is a full next-fiscal-year target for a product still ramping, with no volume shown in hand (e4689 says Vera 'will be deployed'; e5044 says Anthropic is only evaluating it). The size of the discount is argued from the outside market pace of about 50% a year (e4687; e4857's $35bn to $170bn, about 48% a year), so a doubling needs large share gains, and from AMD's competitive benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); the adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) is weighed the other way. The system text sanctions discounting a target with no volume in hand, and any discount below 2x puts the outcome in rung 3, so the band is checkable against the passages; the exact 85% is the writer's judgement. Equating the FY2027 $20bn CPU figure and the doubling with Vera CPU revenue rests on e4856's and e4691's passages, which tie both to Vera, and is stated in reasons. One loose sentence: the move condition 'if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion' would not change a forecast that already assumes a $20bn base.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread2-packets/T15.codex.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"The forecast applies an estimated 85% growth rate to the $20 billion FY2027 base (e3660, e4856), yielding $37 billion in rung 3. NVIDIA's doubling outlook (e4691, e4856) anchors the estimate. Broad planned deployments (e4689) support growth above the outside market benchmarks (e4687, e4857). Anthropic's evaluation (e5044) gives no order volume, and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) support allowing for competition as Vera ramps.\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\nreread2-packets/T17.claude.json\n1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F4, the writer's own 85% estimate of FY2027 Vera revenue as a share of the $20 billion FY2027 CPU guidance (e3660), giving F5 = $17bn, combined with F6, the writer's own estimate of exactly 2x growth (benchmarked on e4856/e4691), giving F7 = $34bn. The estimates rest on e4689, e5044, e5400 and e5403.\", \"why_not\": \"Rung 3 comes entirely from the 15% cut in F4. The same arithmetic without it gives $40bn (F3). The passages say FY2028 will be more than double the $20bn (e3660, e4856, e4764), which means above $40bn, in rung 2. No passage sizes the cut or gives a reason for it. e3660 restates a current-year expectation ('still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue'), and the writer recasts it as an 'opportunity'. No passage shows any non-Vera CPU revenue or any shortfall against the $20bn. e4689 (deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating) argue against a cut, not for one. e5400 and e5403 are AMD's own benchmark claims and say nothing about sales. F6 takes exactly 2x where the guidance says 'more than double', so both assumptions lean down without a passage that quantifies either. The stated reason for the 85% ('the evidence gives no sales volumes') is missing confirmation, and the system text says that alone is no grounds. The system text does allow a default discount for a product with no shipments yet, but no passage shows Vera is not shipping, and the rung depends on the size of the cut, which nothing in the evidence supports.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread2-packets/T17.codex.json\n5:      \"ground\": \"The $34 billion forecast assumes Vera realizes 85% of NVIDIA’s $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook (e3660) and then doubles in FY2028, using management’s growth outlook as a benchmark (e4691, e4856).\",\n6:      \"why_not\": \"Expected broad deployment (e4689) and Anthropic’s evaluation (e5044) indicate interest but give no purchase volumes or Vera revenue share. At the assumed doubling rate, Vera must realize at least 75% and less than 100% of the FY2027 CPU outlook to land in rung 3. The passages do not establish that range, and AMD’s benchmarks (e5400, e5403) do not quantify the effect on Vera sales.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
