{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && nl -ba S/user.txt | sed -n '86,141p' && for f in S/answers/claude.json S/answers/codex.json; do rg -n 'reference_for.*v6|reference_unavailable|\\\"id\\\": \\\"F1\\\"' \"'$f; done; nl -ba ../../../docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'138,158p'\"","aggregated_output":"    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    87\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    90\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n    95\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    96\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    97\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n    98\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n    99\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   106\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   115\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   116\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   118\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   120\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   125\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   126\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.4:      \"id\": \"F1\",\n153:      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\n389:      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n4:      \"id\": \"F1\",\n20:      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\n256:      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   138\t- \"Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n   139\t\n   140\t**The system text:** one change. \"a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario\" becomes \"a quantity the family shares\n   141\thas one forecast\". Every other sentence is unchanged.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 4. Build\n   144\t\n   145\t- **New files.** `calc_small.py` holds `from_answer(data, family)` (wire to internal, with S6/S7 marker errors), `check`,\n   146\t  `publish_decision`, `evaluate`, `upstream_of` and `key`, and returns `{errors, recorded, published}` as `calc_check` does.\n   147\t  `render_v13.v13s_schema` builds the schema; `judging-v13s.txt` holds the system text.\n   148\t- **The plan.** `paid_gate.item` takes a format. A v13s plan (`paid-plan-v13s.json`, pinned) uses `calc_small`. A plan without a\n   149\t  format uses `calc_check` and `strip`, so the old run's `results.json` reproduces byte for byte on a copy.\n   150\t- **Rehearsal and mutants.** The rehearsal takes the plan path. Its stub answers are written in v13s. Each rule S1–S8 gets a mutant.\n   151\t- **Size.** Characters and estimated tokens for the whole request, before and after, per item. The schema is expected to be smaller\n   152\t  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\n   153\t\n   154\t**Value × reader.**\n   155\t- `from_answer`'s output is read by `check`, `publish_decision`, `merge`, `card_view` and `normalise`. It uses the internal names\n   156\t  those readers read (`evidence`, `inputs`, `rests_on`, `outcome`, `reference`), and the dropped keys never appear.\n   157\t- `merge`'s unification of a shared forecast uses `evaluate`'s values with no bound.\n   158\t- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"agent_message","text":"**The FAIL is real. Ask Alex what evidence is enough to publish a forecast before requesting the paid re-send.** The decisive case is the $36B Vera forecast. Its writer identified a risk to sales, then assigned that risk a $4B cost. Neither reader found evidence for the amount. That one answer fails the registered test regardless of how the four disagreements are resolved. [E writer’s assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n\n1. **Serious — U05 deserves its settled “no.”** Management expected FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double a $20B base. The writer used $40B as its reference and forecast 90% realization. The deployment announcement supports demand. Anthropic’s evaluation and AMD’s benchmarks establish competitive risk. None gives the timing or volume of sales likely to slip. Calling $4B “less than half an average quarter” expresses the same assumption in another unit. It adds no evidence for it. At exactly $40B the answer enters the higher band, so the unsupported cut decides the result. **Wording correction:** the result report says four readers rejected U05. The record has two first readers, in agreement, and no adjudication. [E request and bands](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) · [Writer’s conclusion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Codex reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.json:5) · [Claude reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.claude.json:1) · [Report wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:41)\n\n2. **Serious — The four splits have different merits.** I read both initial readings and both adjudications for each. All eight adjudicator votes followed their own family. My readings below leave the recorded splits intact. [Vote tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n\n   | Reply | My reading | What decides it |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | **U02 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on support. The range is valid.** | The writer applies 98% to $720B and gets $705.6B. Its own 96% lower share gives $691.2B. Crossing $700B is allowed: the checker actually publishes $690.9–720.3B, and the schema permits selecting an overlapping band with a reason. The weakness is the claimed independent floor. If all of the cited **more than $730B** of AI infrastructure really is data-center capex for these firms, it exceeds even the writer’s **total** spending midpoint of $720B. The writer needed to reconcile those estimates before using one to validate the other. [Writer’s reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Checked range](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [Schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/schema.json:334) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.claude.json:1) |\n   | **U04 · S unresolved control** | **Codex “yes” is better supported.** | At $745B total, the four firms cross $700B at a data-center share of about 94%. The control names that boundary and addresses the strongest contrary passage. The $730B figure names the right four companies but measures AI infrastructure. The nearly $800B data-center figure covers five companies. Neither establishes how much of the four firms’ spending qualifies. Claude has a plausible reason to lean upward. Its claim that the first figure supplies a floor requires a relationship the passage leaves open. That makes this a defensible abstention and a poor control for an *undisputed* abstention. [Control](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [Four-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Five-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.claude.json:1) |\n   | **U06 · E forecast** | **Codex “no.” The existing definition settles the error in Claude’s justification.** | Keep the writer’s 90% Vera share and run its proposed lower growth rate. **$20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B**, one band below the claimed $30–40B. Claude effectively substitutes a 100% share to obtain $30B. The definition requires judging the answer with the writer’s own assumptions. Claude is right on one factual detail: e4856 does connect Vera to the $20B potential. That passage still supplies neither the writer’s realized share nor a floor for NVIDIA’s growth equal to the whole market’s. The writer’s $32.4B arithmetic is correct. The reader’s claim about the whole interval is wrong. [Writer’s reasoning and sensitivity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Vera passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:27) · [Definition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1) |\n   | **U08 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on the claimed bound. This is the closest call on the forecast itself.** | At $745B total, spending outside data centers can be at most $45B for the forecast to clear $700B. The writer deducts $25B and explicitly allows some AI infrastructure spending outside data centers. Claude’s comparison implies less than $15B **outside AI**. It leaves the amount **outside data centers** open on the writer’s own terms. Meta’s roughly 4% residual offers a concrete comparison, but the writer gives no reason the combined residual of all four firms stays below $45B. Alex’s open question bears directly on whether that comparison nevertheless supports a most-likely forecast. [Writer’s deduction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.claude.json:1) |\n\n3. **Serious — The recurring writer failure is assigning a size to a risk when that size selects the answer. The recurring reader disagreement is how much a comparison can support.** Counting each original reply once, excluding controls, gives the following. For run three I used its last authorized reading. A joint reply counts once when both forecasts were supported. [Third-run votes by reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:92) · [Third-run usable replies by case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:198) · [Targeted-run votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n\n   | Original replies | Settled yes | Settled no | Split |\n   |---|---:|---:|---:|\n   | Third run, 12 | 8 | 2 | 2 |\n   | Targeted run, 6 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n   | **Total, 18** | **10** | **3** | **5** |\n\n   Across **eight S and E replies**, one had settled support, three had settled rejection, and four split. All eight forecast a number. The clearest recurrence is the E Codex writer cutting the guidance reference by 15% in run three and 10% after the request explicitly asked for evidence sizing the cut. All eight P-family replies across the runs had settled support. O accounts for the remaining yes and split, on whether $18B described funding of an existing commitment balance. The stored votes therefore establish three rejected replies, not eight writer errors. They also show reader variation: the *same* third-run S Codex answer went **no → yes → no** across its three readings. [Earlier E cut](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T17.codex.json:5) · [Revised request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:9) · [O disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T02.codex.json:5) · [S original vote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:494) · [First re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:291) · [Second re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:123)\n\n4. **Serious — Alex settled the duty to justify an adjustment. How strong an indirect comparison must be is still his decision.** D741 requires the deduction to be stated or a supported bound given. D745 accepts an unresolved answer when its reason survives the strongest passages. The writer is also told to choose the most likely outcome and to size estimates from comparable deals, capacity, units, selling time, or price. The frozen examples illustrate the distinction. On q04, the reader used roughly $650B of plans and evidence about demand, costs, and delays to forecast $650–720B. On q19, two selling quarters and a $5,000 CPU price made the four million units implied by guidance assessable. No passage supplied the final discount. Those examples support reasoned estimates. They neither establish S’s category relationship nor provide the missing sales evidence for FY2028 Vera. [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [D745](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11510) · [Writer’s sizing instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:4) · [q04 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [q19 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897)\n\n5. **Minor — Two details change how to interpret the paid option.** Its third reader is Codex. A published singleton stops the test if a reader-confirmed unsized adjustment decides its band. The free test asked about *support*, so these votes indicate a risk, not a measured paid-stop rate. Also, paid S asks about **2027** while targeted S asks about **2026**. Finally, the report’s statement that an unresolved singleton is automatically held is inaccurate. I replayed all nine stored answers and controls in memory. Both unresolved controls returned `publish` with no numeric outcome. Such a card has no chosen band, but the paid verdict does inspect published singleton cards for defects. [Paid reading and stop rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Paid cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43) · [Targeted cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:166) · [Singleton publication rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Paid verdict code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n\n**One recommendation.** Put this question to Alex: **“When a related figure makes one band more likely, but the evidence still leaves the next band plausible, may we publish the forecast if we explain why the comparison applies, or should we say ‘unresolved’ until the evidence bounds the quantity on the deciding side?”** I favor allowing the forecast when the writer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period, and quantity asked about, and why it favors that side of the cutoff. U05 fails even that reading.\n\nThat ruling costs no provider spend. A fresh read under unchanged instructions would sample the same disagreement. A code or request change now would spend engineering and review time implementing one side before Alex chooses it. Closing S and E wholesale costs both forecasts whenever the evidence later becomes sufficient. The 36-call paid re-send would measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. It cannot settle what Alex wants published. The earlier plan estimated **$0.17** for first attempts at typical output length under a **$0.50** ceiling; a new send requires separate authorization. [Registered sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375) · [Paid cost assumptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:29)\n\n**One test.** After Alex rules, register one **$0 blinded contrast**. Give fresh writers the same spending question twice. One version has only the indirect comparison Alex ruled on. The other adds a clearly labeled test passage that explicitly bounds the four firms’ data-center spending above $700B and below $800B. Fix the expected decisions from Alex’s answer before showing either version. Mix in the saved U05 as the unsupported-cut control. The result moves the decision only if both reader families apply Alex’s rule to the indirect case, accept a forecast on the explicitly bounded case, and reject U05’s unsized cut. Agreement would justify returning to writer verification under a settled standard. Another split would keep the paid request closed. The original D745 FAIL stands either way."}}
